          <!-- ====== EPIGRAPH ====== -->
          <section id="epigraph" class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-title">T. S. Eliot</h2>

            <blockquote><p>I confess, however, that I am not myself very much concerned with the question of influence, or with those publicists who have impressed their names upon the public by catching the morning tide and rowing very fast in the direction in which the current was flowing; but rather that there should always be a few writers preoccupied in penetrating to the core of the matter, in trying to arrive at the truth and to set it forth, without too much hope, without ambition to alter the immediate course of affairs, and without being downcast or defeated when nothing appears to ensue.</p></blockquote>
          </section>

          <!-- ====== LETTER ====== -->
          <section id="editor-letter" class="content-section" data-letter>
            <p class="rd-essay-n">1 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Letter from the Editor</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Jake Meador</p>

            <blockquote><p>letter from the editor</p></blockquote>
            <p>One of my personal heroes is a German pastor and theologian named Martin Bucer, who was a contemporary of Luther and John Calvin at the dawn of the Reformation. Bucer will confound your expectations–he was relentlessly eager to befriend his fellow Christians and had an unconquerable hopefulness about him, even in the face of deep schism in the western church. Along with Luther’s friend Philip Melanchthon, he was a champion of Christian unity and dedicated much of his ministry to a principled ecumenism.</p>
            <p>It being the 16th century, Bucer’s travels were by necessity slow and laborious and, sometimes, quite dangerous. Typically he would have followed Germany’s many rivers, but at times he would out of necessity be drawn into the dense forests, places where one could easily get lost or be attacked by bandits or even by wolves, though presumably Bucer traveled in large enough groups that those risks were mitigated. Even so, the forest itself could have a haunted, ethereal quality. Go read Goethe’s <em>Erlkönig</em> and you’ll pick up on some of that menace. Better still, listen to Schubert’s musical setting–you can find plenty of examples online to listen to if you desire.</p>
            <p>I sometimes wonder what it might have been like to do pastoral ministry in such a world, to say nothing of the travel and complex personal mediation Bucer did in his pursuit of promoting love and unity amongst Christians.</p>
            <p>For all the challenges that came with that world, of course, and I have no desire to minimize any of them, it also strikes me that one of the greatest differences between his time and ours is found by considering how attention must have worked for them. I do not think it’s true to say we are “busier” today than someone like Bucer would have been. He was plenty busy. He and his equally remarkable wife Elisabeth had at least six children, perhaps more–the record is unclear. They also opened their home to seminarians, who at that time lived with the family of the local minister who they also studied with, and to refugees from France. (The most famous French refugee to visit them in Strasbourg, of course, was John Calvin, who would go on to be mentored by Bucer and who was introduced to his wife by Bucer.) Other ministers of the era had a similar practice. Martin and Katie Luther lived in the old Augustinian monastery in Wittenberg precisely so that they would have the space needed for their family and their many guests. Their lives were taken up with reading, sermon preparation, pastoral care (Bucer wrote what I believe is the first manual by a Protestant minister on soul care), extensive correspondence, and the ordinary work of family life. They were most certainly <em>busy.</em> But I have to think the way they attended to their extensive work must have been quite different than ours.</p>
            <p>Imagine Bucer riding to meet with other Protestant leaders for a conference to try and draft a common Protestant confession of faith to present to the emperor. He would have left his home and rode for several days by the river before plunging into the forest en route to Marburg. He would have needed to attend to his horse, obviously, and to pay attention to the road. He would have conversed with his fellow travelers. Perhaps they sang songs as they traveled. Perhaps he prayed. But during those days of travel, that would have been about all he did. He couldn’t speak to Elisabeth or their children. He couldn’t write a sermon. He couldn’t have a call with a parishioner. Everything he had to attend to was physically near him and he simply lacked the means to pay attention to anything else, save in his thoughts. I can’t help imagining that one would do one’s work in quite a different way, given such an experience. At the very least, I imagine he showed up to Marburg in better spirits than I often am at the end of a long and busy day. (So much the better for him, as he would then spend the next several days being berated by Martin Luther!)</p>
            <p>Though this issue of the journal is not purely or deliberately devoted to the one question of attention, it is a core concern that runs through many of the pieces you will find inside. In different ways, Paul Pastor, Charles Cotherman, and Emily Carrington are all asking us, in their pieces, to pay attention to the good things God has surrounded us with and to consider those things worthy of our attention and of our delight. Other pieces, like Nadya Williams’s review of Bonnie Kristian and Kristin Kobes Du Mez’s recent books, as well as Kyle Williams’s review of a new reader on Christian political economy, challenge us to attend more closely to the historic wisdom of Scripture and the Church, which are too easily forgotten in our day. Jaime Smith’s powerful essay on infertility will challenge you to attend more carefully to how you speak about family life and also to attend more closely to the pain experienced by many of our brothers and sisters. Samuel James’s review of a recent book on technology will encourage you to greater thoughtfulness about language and about how we make arguments. Finally, we are publishing our first ever short story in the journal, which is a dystopian tale by Katy Carl that incorporates issues of family life, food, and work. One thread in the story concerns the inattentiveness of the characters to their world–until it is too late.</p>
            <p>There are other essays to enjoy as well. Daniel Williams’s review of a recent book on American church history gave me a new way of understanding aspects of American Protestantism. Joseph Griffith's offers a sharp treatment of the deceptively tricky idea of “parental rights.” Kirsten Sanders has a punchy and perceptive review of Du Mez which runs on somewhat parallel tracks to Nadya’s double review. Matthew Loftus considers ten lies of therapy culture and what truths we should believe instead. We also have poetry from Mischa Willett and an excellent reflection on <em>The Odyssey</em> from Sarah-Jane Murray.</p>
            <p>When Bucer traveled through the forests of Germany, what kept him safe was the band of friends he traveled with. Bandits and wolves were far less likely to attack groups, particularly groups that had armed guards with them, as Bucer almost certainly would have given his position. What protected him from the dangers of his day, in other words, was the presence of other people he trusted, who helped to keep the danger at bay. My hope for this issue of the magazine is that all of us might find something similar applies to us in our own day. I am not threatened by robbers or predators when I travel from one place to another. But in all I do I am tempted toward distraction and the superficiality that our distracted state makes nearly unavoidable. If we are to be protected from the dangers of our day, it is likely that it will be through the same means that Bucer himself was protected. So thank you for being part of our band of friends. I hope your attending to this issue of the <em>Mere Orthodoxy</em> journal is rich, encouraging, and instructive.</p>
            <p class="letter-sig">Jake Meador</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio contributor-bio--letter">Jake Meador is the editor-in-chief of Mere Orthodoxy. He lives in his hometown of Lincoln, NE with his wife and four children.</p>

    <a class="rd-next" href="#the-tactics-of-delight"><span class="rd-next-label">Next essay</span><span class="rd-next-title">The Tactics of Delight</span></a>
</section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 1 ====== -->
          <section id="the-tactics-of-delight" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">2 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The Tactics of Delight</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Paul J. Pastor</p>

            <blockquote><p>[F]rom it entire forests can spring and may yet cover the earth.</p><p><em>- Michael D. O’Brien</em></p></blockquote>
            <p>In a recurring dream that came often in childhood, a dream that returns every few years, I am in the back field of the little red farmhouse in which I lived as a boy. The machine shed stands behind me, full of shadows and the smells of ancient motor oil. Ahead of me is the sheep paddock, wet with the fitful rain of June, the grass bent under the water beaded on its many seeded heads. In my hand is a vintage folding lawn chair (aluminum, nylon webbing). I unfold it, knowing suddenly, that I am asleep but in my favorite dream, and delighted at what I know will come next. I open the chair with that wonderful <em>snap,</em> sit, and take off into the air.</p>
            <p>In the chair I take silent, swift flight. I glide over the acreage where I grew up, viewing from above the cherry and apple orchards, the ramshackle fences, the tangles and thickets and all. As I look down, I think a million thoughts: <em>what a good old walnut tree that is—I have never fully appreciated it before,</em> thoughts like, <em>oh there is Annie the gray tabby cat! What an absolutely wonderful cat she is!,</em> thoughts like <em>the pears will be ripe soon, and how wonderful that will be.</em> As I fly, I am overwhelmed with a feeling that it is my love for these things that allows me to fly; it is my pure joy in them. Should that leave, I will find that I am only me again, a normal me sitting in an old chair, heavy and incapable of flight.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>Something has happened to joy. While real inequities and challenges abound, it is safe to say that few cultures in history have had so many of the apparent ingredients of happiness as ours with so little joy to show for it. Deaths of despair (classed as suicide, overdoses, and deaths from alcohol-related liver damage) have doubled over the past twenty years. The birth rate of developed nations is declining precipitously. The young are, suddenly, less happy than the old. There is the sense, even in the face of evidence to the contrary, that everything is simply getting worse. Worst of all, many of us feel that there is nothing that can be done about any of it.</p>
            <p>Ours has become a culture of “decadence without pleasure,” say Michèle Mendelssohn and Charlie Tyson. The wheels are coming off, and the crash is not even any <em>fun.</em> Younger generations are experiencing profound nostalgia for a pre-Internet life that they have never known. Circana, a leading market research and analytics group, noted recently that one of the key trends across essentially all discretionary buying categories (clothing, cosmetics, books, toys, games, food and beverage) is <em>nostalgia.</em> In their analysis, retro-styled promotions performed better than better-value deals at restaurants, showing that people are consistently willing to pay more for food that feels “old.” This is, if you really think about it, ridiculous. Also, <em>I totally get it.</em> I think I’d pay extra for a burger that made me feel like it was 1989 again. It wouldn’t even have to taste better.</p>
            <p>Yes, something has happened to joy, at every level of our society, from the demographic to the diner. I feel it. You might feel it too. And I do not think that this is accidental.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>In the Christian story, there is a pressure behind all other pressures; a dark Power that unifies all expressions of dark power. It is typified by the city of “Babylon,” an archetypal civilization that transcends and founds what the Bible calls “the world.” Even in Biblical literature, “Babylon” includes multiple imperial nations: Babel/Ur, Media, Persia, Babylon proper, Greece, Rome. Babylon is a way of being. That way of being is a culture of perfected and absolute <em>transaction.</em> When the angel coming to St. John calls Babylon “the great whore,” this is not about sex at all, it is about <em>selling what ought to be pure gift.</em> She is in actuality not a woman at all, but a “great city, which reigneth over the kings of the earth.”</p>
            <p>In my reading, this is quite clear. There is an animating spirit of total transaction, which seeks total and perfect power over the earth and all her peoples. This spirit is what rules the rulers of the world, who in seeking profit and power, are not serving themselves at all, but Babylon. It is that evil civilization and way of being whose principles of operation are immediately recognizable regardless of time or setting, and who kills, without thought or regret, anyone who threatens the scope or efficiency of her power of merchandise, a merchandise that counts as equal everything from “cinnamon” to the “souls of men” (Revelation 18:11-13). In Babylon, nothing is sacred. Everything is for sale.</p>
            <p>This weight of possession is an enormous pressure on living things. It drains joy in the most horrible and spiritually serious way. There is a reason in English that <em>free</em> means both without cost and without owner. The opposite of <em>free</em> is the entanglements of price. To be truly free is to be priceless, not of <em>a very high price</em> but <em>of no price at all.</em> This, above all else, is hateful to Babylon and the Babylonian mind. The existence of things without price is a terrifying indictment of their entire way of being. It is an unfathomably dangerous idea that always draws the wrath of Babylonian people and powers. If true, even in its smallest sense, then the Empire has no clothes.</p>
            <p>Our moment in history, through the advent of exotic technology, has brought Babylon to a means of efficiency and totality never before been seen in history, and shows little signs of slowing. This pressure, behind all other pressures, is building to levels we have yet to understand. It represents unprecedented pressures <em>animated solely by greed for profit and power,</em> pressures that are crushing free human behavior, traditional ways of life, human culture and creativity, the integrity of natural ecosystems, and every other possible imagination of the free, the wild, the beautiful, the sacred, and the true. The wild is the enemy of Babylon, because it is inefficient or impossible to sell. Only the consumer is sacred to the Babylonian mind. Because of the overwhelming idolatry of this mode of being, William Stringfellow wrote (in <em>Imposters of God)</em> of the “ubiquity of alienation” that results from this vast chaos, of “men and of institutions and of all creatures … existing in a state of estrangement each from all the others.” It is in this atomized breakdown of the human relationship to all things that Babylon is able to feast. Everything is to be fed to that eternal and infernal hunger.</p>
            <p>Everything.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>Say that a very strong spring is compressed from above by a very great weight, a weight that keeps pushing and pushing and pushing the spring into a smaller and smaller space. Sooner or later, one of two things will happen. The spring will break, or it will blow out sideways.</p>
            <p>People and physics have a great deal in common. Nearly all the revolutions and radical social movements of history could be viewed through the lens of “pressed people blowing out sideways.” It does not matter if the pressure is real or perceived. This is just something that people do. People were not made to be crushed. The <em>sideways</em> nature of this is important. It is asymmetrical by nature. Surprises happen, suddenly. Before the blowout, the blowout is unthinkable. Afterwards, very smart people all agree that the blowout was the only thing that possibly could have happened.</p>
            <p>The word <em>radical</em> is famously rooted in the Latin <em>radix</em> for (yes, thank you) “root.” It took on its present usage of <em>extreme</em> or <em>uncompromising</em> around the early 1800s when British politicians began calling for <em>radical reforms,</em> meaning to rip up certain social problems “by the root.” I think “radical” is a wonderful word. <em>Radical.</em> I love it the way I love pulling up invasive Himalayan blackberries from my garden. I pull on the big thick leather gloves, the ones that keep most of the thorns out. I plant my feet and I pull and pull until the big woody taproot, with its little white tender hair-roots clinging to the soil, begins to loosen from the ground, then all at once comes forth. To pull up a hateful thing by the root is simply wonderful. There is no agreement, no truce. There is no compromise.</p>
            <p>People who have nothing to lose become free in a way that is unique in modern society. They are free to be uncompromising and desperate. Such people realign like molecules under great pressure. They either crack or (suddenly turning to new angles) they become rigid and clear and sharp, like crystals. There would be, I imagine, a sort of rush, a <em>glee</em> to that; a feeling that one could move a mountain perhaps—or walk on water.</p>
            <p>The pressures of our moment are overwhelming, but largely hidden. The conditions of contemporary life have essentially solved perennial problems of physical survival, but have, in the words of Byung Chul-Han in <em>The Burnout Society,</em> taken “the <em>livingness</em> from life.” The world that we have made has become essentially dishonest, and thus unkind. Our “achievement society” has created a different and lesser variety of human than those even of recent memory, a sort of human being who is “too alive to die, and too dead to live.” This is an unspeakable pressure, and one felt <em>at every level of society.</em></p>
            <p>For Han, radicalization takes the form of “tiredness,” a state that he emphasizes is the foundation, oddly, of a “Pentecostal community,” a community that in opposition to the crushing smoothness and efficiency of our dominant consumer culture is able to be filled with the Spirit of Life. In this, it is able to enter a community of belonging, a community of friendship with all things. We join in “savoring” our exhaustion. This is a form of radicalization that casts off the overwhelming vanity of human domination.</p>
            <p>The idea that Christianity offers an alternative both to the crushing of Babylon and to violence as a response to its pressure is nothing new. It is intrinsic to the faith, what C.S. Lewis in <em>Mere Christianity</em> calls an invitation “to take part in a great campaign of sabotage,” or what the present Pope Leo has termed “holy rebellion.”</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>Joy, in the literature of the Bible, is connected intimately to both strength and freedom. To be joyful is to be capable of something. Joy is both the source and the result of a powerful capacity, a capacity whose ultimate source is in God. This is a power that grants refuge and protection: in translating <em>the joy of the Lord is your strength</em> from the Book of Nehemiah, we might replace “strength” with “fortress.” We might almost replace it with “power source.” Think also of the anthropomorphized sun in Psalm 19, who <em>rejoiceth as a strong man.</em> It is the presence of the Spirit of Life that confers this strength, as is obvious from Galatians 5, where that Spirit is able to set the Christian absolutely free, so free that one’s life is defined no longer by the desires of the flesh, but by the law of love. In God’s presence is <em>fullness of joy</em> (Psalm 16), a joy that carries a sense of absolute liberation.</p>
            <p>This brings us to an interesting point. Joy, in the vast, cosmic, ideal, abstract sense is, for humans at least, intimately connected to joys—small and concrete instances of delight, pleasure, and praise. Joy and joys exist in a delicate symbiosis. While joy (cosmic) is certainly the chicken, her eggs (joys) contain in each of them her larger, if undeveloped form. Thus the species is propagated. The Psalmist himself in the last verse of Psalm 16 makes the gentle distinction: in God’s presence is “fullness of joy,” at his right hand “are pleasures forevermore.” Both of these, perhaps, may be read as “the path of life.”</p>
            <p>This accords perfectly with my experience in the matter, and likely with yours. Is it not true that joyful people surround themselves with joys? Is it also not true that, in a wonderful feedback loop, those joys promote the original joy? Do not delights surround the person who takes delight in their life? To the one who has, more will indeed be given. The free surround themselves with freedoms; blessings follow the blessed; it is not a meaningless thing to be called “lucky.”</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>The Book of Revelation is the biblical book most overtly concerned with the conflict between Babylon and the People of God. The book is hallucinogenic; wonderful; grotesque. It is the stuff of Bosch and childhood monster drawings. The conflict is stated in terms of “overcoming”—sixteen times in the book that word is used of one or the other of the combatants getting the upper hand. It is very exciting.</p>
            <p>Of special weight is Revelation 12:11, where a loud voice from heaven calls out:</p>
            <blockquote><p><em>Now is come salvation, and strength, and the kingdom of our God, and the power of his Christ: for the accuser of our brethren is cast down, which accused them before our God day and night.</em></p></blockquote>
            <blockquote><p><em>And they overcame him by the blood of the Lamb, and by the word of their testimony; and they loved not their lives unto the death.</em></p></blockquote>
            <p>In this passage is set in plain sight the method by which Babylon and her master may be cast down forever. The “blood of the Lamb,” meaning the total salvation effected by the absolute pouring out of God’s own life through the death of Jesus, and “the word of their testimony.” These things, paired with a total commitment (the saints would rather die than forsake their Lord) are <em>how</em> the people of God overcome.</p>
            <p>Focus for a moment on “the word of their testimony.” The Greek seems simple at first: <em>τὸν λόγον τῆς μαρτυρίας αὐτῶν.</em> But the two key words here are capacious words. “Word” is <em>logos,</em> which may be a simple word, but was also used to mean <em>pattern, doctrine,</em> or <em>reason.</em> It is our direct source of “logic” in English. Similarly, “testimony” is <em>marturias,</em> meaning <em>witness,</em> and our direct root of “martyr.” It is not too much to say that this may not be limited to a verbal testimony. It could be well translated as the <em>logic of their radical witness,</em> their life’s “word” that speaks of true reality.</p>
            <p>I make this point for a simple reason. There is more than one way for a faithful Christian to give their live as an overcomer, as a radical martyr. Much is rightly made of those of us thrown to lions, or shot in shallow graves, or bound to crosses in the sand at low tide. But there are lifelong deaths no less heroic, no less effective in the word of their testimony—the potent logic of their radical witness.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>Here is the secret: We may “overcome” Babylon by means of joy. For those of us learning to dwell in the presence of God, joys will begin to multiply around us, even in the most hostile conditions. This, if we are aware of it, is a sideways blowout. It is the most wonderful sort of slow explosion, able to upend even the most crushing pressures of Babylon and the dark powers behind her. Joy, the holy weapon able to effect what no bullet or vote or revolution ever could; joys, the means to deliver this weapon in uncountable places, across every one of our days. We are Christ’s own guerrillas, at a time of profound crisis. And this is the moment to press our strange advantage.</p>
            <p>I have taken some trouble to write all this because I hope that it will help us counter the feelings of helplessness that so naturally arise in the face of Babylonian power. You feel it crushing you, do you not? You feel it crushing all that is alive, and free, and whole, and pure, and good. You feel it trying to dig to the roots of the world, and it is pissing poison poison poison all the way, all empty money and puffy pride and it will end in blood, it always ends in blood. <em>But what can I do?</em> We say to ourselves. <em>I cannot touch it. I cannot hurt it.</em> Oh, but you can touch it. You can hurt it. Likely you did, even today, but you did not realize it. You hurt the huge and monstrous thing by means of joys. The enemy is spiritual, but its evil is felt in a million little physical ways. Very well then, return to the “deeper magic.” Good works the same way, and in Christ is becoming infinite. You are part of that infinity, and so am I, and out from us go spilling loaves of fresh crusty bread, and little kind words to the downcast, and goodhearted jokes, and gardens and <em>La Sagrada Familia,</em> and whole books of hymns and poetry and nursery rhymes and geeky stories about Hobbits, and <em>you have a part to play,</em> you do.</p>
            <p>And so my friend, I appeal to you to play it. Allow yourself to become radicalized, totally and completely, in service of what will, in the end, overcome that which is degrading and destroying that which you truly love. Commit yourself to a course of action from which there is no turning back; love not your life unto death. Renew your love for the blood of the Lamb, but seek, in all honesty, what the <em>logic of your radical witness</em> may be. What is the pattern of <em>your</em> testimony? Perhaps it is with words, or perhaps it not mostly with words, but in either case, it will be grounded in the presence of God, and it will result in a multiplication of joys.</p>
            <p>To what are we bearing witness? To Christ, who is “trampling down death by death,” and leading us into everlasting life. This presence may come for us, as it did to the gathered Upper Room at Pentecost, in the form of a profound and radical exhaustion with the world as we have been told that it must be, a radicalization which may lead to the explosion of life in ways that are presently unthinkable but which may, in hindsight, be said by all the pundits to have been the only thing that possibly could have happened.</p>
            <p>Joy! I say then, <em>Joy!</em> Joy our battle cry and joy our comfort to the weary. Joy in “ten thousand places,” joy in our going out and our coming in, in our rising and our lying down. Joy, <em>radical and uncompromising;</em> joy that can level kingdoms. Joy that will remain uneroded when every pyramid is dust, joy whose radiation does not decay, which will live to see children play with what were once rods of plutonium. Joy against the algorithm, joy upending politics, joy that puts the billionaires to shame. Joy that is the joy of the Lord. Joy! Joy!</p>
            <p>To live in this way, I find that I must capture something of the logic of that recurring dream from childhood. It is love and praise and delight that grants the impossible wonder. It is seeing the nature of things, so simple and so grave. It is overcoming by living (even if only for a moment and in what seems like sleep) according to the “word” of a different place, a logic so radical that one can, if one only trusts completely, fly above the world, laugh freely and forever at the very idea of weight.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Paul J. Pastor is an executive editor for Nelson Books, a writer of essays, poetry, and fiction, and the author, among other books, of <em>The Locust Years: Poems</em>, and <em>The Crystal Hand: Poems</em>. He holds a master of arts in exegetical theology from Western Seminary, and a master of fine arts in poetry from the University of St. Thomas. He lives in Oregon.</p>

