          <section id="when-fertility-becomes-a" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">3 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">When Fertility Becomes a Measure of Morality</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Jaime Marie Smith</p>

            <p>The prophet Samuel’s birth came about in a somber and miraculous way. His mother Hannah was barren, despite her overwhelming desire for children. She lived faithfully, worshipped regularly, and still suffered great sorrow for many years filled with unanswered prayers before the Lord eventually opened her womb.</p>
            <p>We’re told that her husband Elkanah loved her greatly and tried to console her, but her grief was too great (1 Samuel 1:5). Elkanah's other wife Peninnah, who had children, treated Hannah with derision because of her barrenness, especially during the family’s annual trips to worship God at Shiloh. One year, Peninnah’s scorn and Hannah’s own deep sorrow caused her to pray so fervently that Eli the High Priest accused her of being intoxicated. Hannah’s beautifully articulate reply to Eli dispelled his accusations while voicing her sorrow more publicly than she was accustomed to doing.</p>
            <p>In Hannah we see the brokenness that accompanies the desire for children in the face of infertility. The birth of Samuel is a poignant reminder that righteousness is not a prerequisite for childbearing, nor is infertility a sign of the Lord’s abandonment. The glory of the Lord was abundantly clear in Hannah’s circumstances precisely because it had nothing to do with whether or not she was loved by her husband, nor whether she was devout in her faith. Samuel was an unmerited gift to a woman who recognized that both in want and in plenty, all she had belonged to the Lord. Because Hannah prayed and suffered for many years before the birth of Samuel, she was intimately aware of the fact that the Lord’s mercy—and not her prayers—were the cause of the blessing she received in the birth of her son.</p>
            <p>And yet, despite the obvious clarity of Scripture on the human condition and our inability to earn the many graces we receive, prosperity gospel theology and its many iterations have long plagued the church. In every generation, even among highly educated circles, there is a temptation to perceive material and familial blessings as rewards for specific forms of faithfulness. Too often we tally up our good works before God and expect a reward while still proclaiming that all we have is unmerited. The wiles of our human nature allow us to reinvent our prosperity gospel and convince ourselves it is nothing of the sort.</p>
            <p>Fertility remains one such idol in the church today. Conservative Christianity exists right now in opposition to the anti-human agenda of the zeitgeist, and this is good. In response, some believers see fertility and procreation as markers of both cultural reclamation and blessing for faithful living. While this has been true for a couple of generations in the American church, the current cultural interest in pro-natalism and a renewed conservative vigor towards increasing the fertility rate has served as a catalyst for conflating political, cultural, and spiritual priorities among Christians.</p>
            <p>I am a woman walking through a recent Stage 4 endometriosis diagnosis and surgery, and more than a year-long struggle with infertility. I have come to see this idolization of fertility on full display—in my own life, in the greater community of the church, and in the culture at large. The unbiblical yet ancient and oh-so-natural belief that fertility can be earned and that it signifies doing things properly infiltrates Instagram feeds and political speeches. It comes from well-intentioned Christians in institutional and personal settings. And it’s in my own heart.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>From the outside, I look like the model conservative woman. I’m a devout Christian. I always dreamed of getting married. I was homeschooled and am open to homeschooling potential children someday. I was chaste until marriage. I married a wonderful man who shares the same vision for life. And, I have always wanted to be a mom.</p>
            <p>But recently, I’ve learned that all of this is not enough. For some who would claim to be compatriots under the auspices of the same broad tent we call conservative Christianity, it isn’t enough to be chaste, pro-family, and desirous of having children. You have to actually be actively procreating (or at least prove that you’re capable of it) in order to be acceptable. The desire for children is in itself a beautiful, natural, and good thing—but increasingly, it is used as a measure of moral inclusion in some conservative spaces.</p>
            <p>One could argue that motherhood has always been a distinct subset of womanhood, a rite of passage that is innate within the bonds of female community. It is frightening, however, that in some conservative communities, not only is there a natural differentiation between women who are mothers and those who are not, there often is a shift towards a sectarian structure wherein mothers (particularly women with many children) are in command and women who are childless are treated (often subconsciously) as socially inferior to those who are already mothers. Instead of communities where women of all different stations value and honor one another, motherhood often becomes the central way for women to establish a sense of belonging within conservative Christian circles.</p>
            <p>Even within cultural Christianity, we have inadvertently made motherhood the prerequisite to being taken seriously. Harrison Butker, in his much-discussed 2024 commencement speech to Benedictine College, told female graduates, “I can tell you that my beautiful wife … would be the first to say that her life truly started when she began living her vocation as a wife and as a mother.” Clearly, it’s admirable that he lauded family values in his speech. But there is also the clear assumption that a woman’s life is incomplete—and that it does not fully begin—until she becomes a mother.</p>
            <p>It’s a worthy desire to elevate the beautiful vocation of motherhood instead of denigrating it as the broader culture has done for decades. But motherhood in 2026 is very much <em>en vogue.</em> Even <em>TIME</em> recently published a piece by a self-proclaimed “queer, progressive mother of two” titled “The Left Must Reclaim Motherhood.” The author, Samantha Mann, contends that “[m] otherhood and joy are not inherently conservative. It’s time for the left (and me) to reclaim this truth—and with it, to imagine a future where parenting is not a battleground but a common ground.”</p>
            <p>In some conservative circles, however, the reactionary pendulum swing back from the girlboss era has left many women with the belief that their worth is closely bound to their desire and ability to birth children, instead of their status as valuable human individuals, loved by God. Of course, no one says this explicitly. But there are plenty of implicit comments made, such as the podcaster Katie Miller’s recent assertion to women that “our biological destiny is to have babies — not slave behind desks chasing careers while our civilization dies.”</p>
            <p>The idea of a “biological destiny” is fundamentally incomplete. Destiny is ontological, not merely biological. Furthermore, the idea that a woman is only fulfilling her true calling as a woman if she bears children undermines the value of womanhood by reducing a woman merely to her procreative abilities.</p>
            <p>Yet the idea of valuing a person based on procreation has infiltrated the mainstream. In a 2021 speech, Vice President J.D. Vance said of people with children: “You should have more of an ability to speak your voice in our democratic republic than people who don’t have kids. Let’s face the consequences and the reality. If you don’t have as much of an investment in the future of this country, maybe you shouldn’t get nearly the same voice.” Instead of assuming that people without children are willful, selfish, unpatriotic, and self-destructing, we need room for those who are struggling, often silent and unseen. Being told you don’t have an investment in the future of the country because you do not have children is a twisted knife to those walking through infertility.</p>
            <p>Another example of the idea that childless couples are increasingly pariahs in society is outlined perfectly by Peechy Keenan, the pseudonymous author of the popular book <em>Domestic Extremist: A Practical Guide to Winning the Culture War.</em> In a 2024 article for <em>The American Mind,</em> she wrote, “People aren’t having enough babies, but it’s mostly liberals choosing to commit genetic suicide. Trads, Christians, Catholics, and other baby-friendly types on the Right are still leaning into parenthood and big families. The libs are staying childfree and for good measure, shooting their kids up with ‘puberty blockers,’ which, in a hilarious plot twist, are actually grandchild blockers. They’re wiping out their own genetic lines on purpose!”</p>
            <p>The presumption that most people who do not have children are liberals who are openly embracing self-demise is a reductionistic and uncharitable reading of the statistics. And for those like me who are want children but struggle with infertility, it feels like a betrayal to read such rebukes, and to be told that our childlessness is laughable “genetic suicide.” Is this what Peninnah told Hannah?</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>Ironically, sexual purity, long held as paramount Christian behavior, is no longer as valuable as procreation to many in the conservative movement. Even though purity culture was an immense force towards sexual purity within conservative Christianity (and, as many have argued, created a true shame culture), we’ve officially entered a brave new world where proven fertility often seems to socially rectify all ills.</p>
            <p>While the church is obviously right to care for and support unmarried couples who are pregnant, it’s striking that in some spaces it’s less <em>socially</em> iniquitous to have a child out of wedlock than it is to be a married childless couple. Perhaps this is because childless couples are too often presumed to be—as Peechy Kennan so clearly espouses—desirous of being childless. Or perhaps (and I assume this might be the more common reason) conservatives are uncomfortable with couples without children because it simply does not fit into the normative Christian experience and therefore feels foreign to communities that are often centered around children. Learning how to truly incorporate childless couples and single people often seems daunting and difficult and is therefore left untried.</p>
            <p>In addition to presumption, we struggle with a limited imagination. While very few people would ever tell you that struggling with infertility is your fault, you can hear them recommending a different diet, telling you to get rid of all clothing with synthetic fibers, suggesting ways to lower the toxic load of your home, and offering the subtle suggestion that your job is making you too stressed. At the end of the day you are, in their mind, doing something incorrectly.</p>
            <p>I am interested in clean living, I pursue it in my own life, and I think it’s advisable. I also recognize that I live in a broken world with a broken body, and while there are natural methods that can improve fertility outcomes, sometimes there is no crunchy remedy for the reality of living in the broken space our humanity occupies.</p>
            <p>Some will read me as a cautionary tale. They will feel sorry for the woman who, because she struggled to bear a child, found it expedient to judge the women who could. Don’t worry—this is not the bitter diatribe of an infertile woman. I love and support other women who are able to be mothers, and I celebrate with my friends who already have known that joy. I have hope for my own health journey, and I trust the Lord’s will for my life. Yet I have noticed that so often, conversations about fertility and motherhood descend into tribalistic arenas wherein one ideological camp reduces the perspective of the other into oblivion.</p>
            <p><em>Yesteryear,</em> one of the most popular books of 2026, has been rightly criticized for thinly veiling the author’s obvious contempt of the conservative tradwife trope under the guise of “satire.” We live in an age where critique without an attempt at understanding is normative. As a conservative Christian woman, I affirm the dignity and beauty of motherhood and wholeheartedly offer my support towards policies that protect children and families more broadly. And as a prospective parent, I want to understand the concerns parents face, and to take them seriously. I’m able to affirm people within conservative Christianity who are in a different season of life. And I hope that respect and understanding can be returned. Conservative Christians with children ought to believe the best about those in their communities who do not have children and welcome them into conversations about matters concerning fertility without reserve or judgement. A childless conservative is not an undercover psyop, and a barren couple is not a threat to the equilibrium of conservative Christianity.</p>
            <p>Still, if you’re not having babies, then to some you seem defective. After all, how could you not help in the fight to outnumber your ideological opponents? The statistics show that approximately 1 in 6 couples struggle with infertility. Approximately 15% of any given population knows the grief of struggling to conceive a child. Fertility—much like the hand you are dealt in a game of cards—is something you are given, not something for which you can take credit. But when we live in socially sectarian communities that are unusually fecund and even more unusually proud of it, we lose sight of reality and begin to rewrite what “normal” looks like.</p>
            <p>When couples easily conceive five, six, or even 10 children without many complications, and are in a space where many other families have had a similar experience, it’s understandable that someone’s inability to conceive might seem implausible. <em>After all,</em> they reason, <em>isn’t childbearing simply a natural biological experience?</em></p>
            <p>Yes, but sometimes our broken biology fails us.</p>
            <p>I now know this failure intimately. My heart breaks for the absence of the children I have not been allowed to carry. Some of my dearest friends come from large families, and my husband and I would love to have several children, if God allows. But the shame surrounding infertility has the potential to become a great divide amongst conservative Christians, because the shift towards the idolization of procreation does not simply affect the sociology of a community, it reshapes how we define moral success—and failure. Children can quickly become beads on an abacus, used to tally the moral and spiritual success of their parents. If we continue in this way, we risk fragmentating under the weight of idolizing something that has always been and always will be a gift to be received—not a reward to be earned.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>One of the more extreme versions of the mindset I describe is the belief that fertility is a sign of God’s specific blessing for right moral action. It has become our trendiest form of prosperity gospel. I have been in the room when someone publicly said to a newly married couple, “We pray the Lord will bless you with many children. And if you honor Him, He will.” Yet some of the most faithful women of the Bible struggled with barrenness. “Many children” was not in the cards for Sarah, Rachel, Hannah, or Elizabeth, among others. Even once the Lord opened their wombs, many of them were still unable to conceive enough children for the modern conservative to be proud. After all, Sarah’s one son and Rachel’s two sons certainly don’t meet the mandate of ideologies like the “Quiverfull” movement.</p>
            <p>Even amongst mainline conservatives however, many who reject the ideology that women should bear as many children as physically possible still inadvertently idolize fertility itself. We have often forgotten that being able to conceive is not a sign of the Lord’s blessing for good behavior, and infertility is not a sign of His displeasure. The result of this idolization of fertility is that we risk having communities full of Leahs, trying to prove their worth through labor pains.</p>
            <p>Perhaps more difficult is the reality that Hagar, Leah, and Peninnah each mocked the infertile woman close to them. Some of this was undoubtedly due to the culture they lived in and the competitive and complicated nature of their relationships to each of the barren women. But in each case, fertility was still a means of control and pride. Peninnah’s cruelty towards Hannah was twofold: she demonstrated a complete lack of compassion, and she engaged in prideful superiority. 1 Samuel 1:7 is frank on this point: “As often as [Hannah] went up to the house of the Lord, [Peninnah] used to provoke her.” Even in a place meant for worship, Hannah was taunted and reminded of her perceived inadequacies. Taking pride in fertility is still just as much of a temptation for some women in our communities today.</p>
            <p>Of course, many mothers who have not struggled with their own fertility are incredibly kind to the women around them who have walked a painful road with infertility. I have experienced immense grace from several women who have children–some of whom have walked through infertility themselves, and others who have not. Still, when the standard for the community in some conservative spaces is designed to honor women with children and see women who are childless as fundamentally incomplete—or at least lacking in real purpose and in a metaphorical holding pattern—there is a clear message that fertility is the metric used to differentiate between those who really belong, and those who are superfluous. And with such distinctions (however subtle they may be) comes shame.</p>
            <p>The generation that grew up feeling stifled and downtrodden by purity culture has exchanged one shame culture for another. But instead of the imposition of (often legitimate, but also often misapplied) shame towards illicit behaviors, the newest scarlet letter is the inability to bear children.</p>
            <p>Just as purity culture arguably stemmed from a well-intentioned desire to instill virtue amongst Millennials after a few decades of watching the culture’s unbridled and pervasive anti-Christian sexual ethic, the Right’s new obsession with fertility expresses legitimate concern regarding the national fertility rate and the broader cultural disregard for the sanctity of human life. But purity culture often veered from a well-intentioned effort to encourage chastity amongst young people into an almost cartoonish condemnation of modern dating and a demonization of any type of affection before marriage.</p>
            <p>So too has our rightful concern over fertility often splintered into an obsession with maximizing fertility and treating infertile people with an incurious suspicion. And the result—just as in purity culture—is shame. Like the many girls who felt “impure” because they kissed a boy they didn’t end up marrying, many couples who are already grieving their infertility are left feeling an additional layer of shame that they have failed to conform to the expectations of their community.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>Since Eden, shame has followed the human heart and found a myriad of ways to burden and condemn believers. Curt Thompson, in his book <em>The Soul of Shame</em> argues that vulnerability is the antidote to shame. This is true of shame caused by sin, and it is also certainly true of the shame that accompanies aspects of life such as fertility. While guilt can certainly be the prompting of the Holy Spirit that leads towards repentance for sin, there is also shame that is not connected to wrongdoing but is nevertheless very real.</p>
            <p>Christians struggling with infertility often feel incredible amounts of shame even though they know infertility is one of the consequences of living in a broken world. Being able to embrace vulnerability about their sorrow and pain allows couples walking through infertility to experience grace even as they grieve the “not yet” season they walk through. Part of the experience of vulnerability is being able to sit with the pain and to believe that the Lord is redeeming it with purpose beyond what we can see in the present moment. I’m a very private person, and writing something of this nature is difficult. But I also believe that if even one struggling couple can feel seen and known through this small act of vulnerability, it is completely worthwhile.</p>
            <p>It is a gift when others remind us: “therefore we do not lose heart. Though outwardly we are wasting away, yet inwardly we are being renewed day by day. For our light and momentary troubles are achieving for us an eternal glory that far outweighs them all. So we fix our eyes not on what is seen, but on what is unseen, since what is seen is temporary, but what is unseen is eternal” (2 Corinthians 2:16-18).</p>
            <p>Yet just as Job’s friends insisted that secret sins were the cause of Job’s suffering, too often well-meaning people imply that those walking through infertility must be to blame. Much like those who “wag their heads” in Psalm 22:7, there is a strange mixture of derisiveness and pity amongst many who simply don’t know how to interact with those struggling with infertility. Our physical infirmities are so often a part of life, yet we wrongly assume that those carrying the marks of a broken world that is awaiting redemption are to blame for their conditions.</p>
            <p>Before Jesus heals a man who was born blind, we see this very situation unfold: “As [Jesus] passed by, he saw a man blind from birth. And his disciples asked him, ‘Rabbi, who sinned, this man or his parents, that he was born blind?’ Jesus answered, ‘It was not that this man sinned, or his parents, but that the works of God might be displayed in him’”(John 9:1-3).</p>
            <p>In the moments before healing a man with a chronic condition, the Lord—without ambiguity—declares that the man’s blindness was not the result of the man’s sin nor the sins of his parents. Instead, the Lord appointed him to walk through suffering so that His glory might be displayed in his life. This appointment is often difficult for us to comprehend. But the Lord never allows suffering to be wasted, and He gives grace to those who walk through circumstances they do not desire.</p>
            <p>So what happens when the church—a haven for the weary and wounded— becomes a place where infertility is nearly unimaginable? When our pews are overflowing with children, couples without children are often perceived as an uncomfortable anomaly. I know firsthand that the church can be one of the most difficult places to confront the reality of infertility. This is true regardless of the political tenor of a place, but in communities where fertility creates social capital, the body of believers is not immune to the temptation to elevate fertility and procreation as a marker of specific holiness and right action.</p>
            <p>The answer is not to collapse into progressive churchmanship or liberal theology, where the natural ends of procreation and parenthood are often (even inadvertently) denigrated, but to make room instead for those whose stories are riddled with the pain and loneliness that comes with infertility. When pastors and congregations are ill-equipped or uncomfortable with the sorrow that accompanies infertility, shame festers and vulnerability becomes more difficult. And those who easily conceive and struggle to understand how it might be difficult for others should nonetheless walk in humility and compassion.</p>
            <p>As Hannah says in her song of praise after the birth of Samuel, “Talk no more so very proudly, let not arrogance come from your mouth; for the Lord is a God of knowledge, and by him actions are weighed” (1 Samuel 2:3). Every church has its Sarahs, Rachels, and Hannahs, and to dignify their experience without judgement or assumption is to take seriously the call to live as the body of Christ.</p>
            <p>I’ve often wondered why I have been called to walk through this sorrow. But perhaps I needed to learn this lesson as much as anyone. I did everything the “right” way, and yet, here I am. I unwittingly believed the false gospel that whispered I could avoid particular sufferings by living righteously. But the Lord has reminded me of the bankruptcy of my own heart, and, to quote the Book of Common Prayer, that “there is no health in me.” Yet in His grace, He called me to this story, and I have already seen His faithfulness in circumstances I did not ask for. I know that He has put my tears in His bottle (Psalm 58:6).</p>
            <p>For now, I’ll continue to be Hannah, crying out, asking for deliverance. And if someday we are given the gift of biological children, this season will be a reminder that fertility is not something I can earn, nor is it a moral judgment I can impose on anyone else who faces a similar journey. Christ in His mysterious mercy allows for circumstances we cannot understand. To trade the truth of the gospel for the lie that we can perform to avoid our humanity is a dangerous exchange. Yet He is also making all things new, and I—along with all creation—wait in expectant hope for that day.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Jaime Marie Smith is the program director of a graduate school. She and her husband reside in southern Michigan.</p>

    <a class="rd-next" href="#do-small-places-matter"><span class="rd-next-label">Next essay</span><span class="rd-next-title">Do Small Places Matter?</span></a>
</section>

