          <section id="do-small-places-matter" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">4 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Do Small Places Matter?</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Charles E. Cotherman</p>

            <p>Do small places matter?</p>
            <p>In my experience, this is one of those questions Christians often answer one way with their words and another way with their actions. We know the right answer, but our hopes and dreams—and the routes of our U-hauls—often point in the opposite direction. Yes, we know God loves small places. Yes, we believe that God is still at work in obscure corners of the country, but no, <em>we</em> don’t want to live there.</p>
            <p>I get it. Like so many who grew up in small-town America with teachers, pastors, and even parents regularly reminding us that the path <em>up</em> was usually the path <em>out</em> of our small towns, I have felt what seemed to be the opposing pulls of love for a place and a people and the desire to do something significant with my life. In most of the narratives I heard during my first twenty years, these two desires were set at odds with each other, and it was easy to tell which should take pride of place in my decision making. I might believe theologically that God was active everywhere, but practically, it seemed like the real work was being done in big, seemingly strategic places.</p>
            <p>It was only in my late twenties, as my wife and I commuted over an hour-and-a-half from the small farmhouse we rented from her great uncle to an urban seminary campus that I realized there might be a problem with the way the American church had prioritized certain kinds of ministry while neglecting others. It was 2010, and of the books we read and the stories we heard about everything from church planting to meaningful ministries of compassion, almost all were based in urban or suburban ministry. As I read these books, I started to realize that this approach to theological education and the wide-scale platforming of the biggest and brightest celebrity pastors was missing something important. It seemed that everywhere I looked, the folks who were influencing the next generation of church leaders had forgotten or overlooked small places and the people and churches who called them home.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Americans (Kind of) Discover Their Rural Neighbors</h3>
            <p>Since then, the situation has changed a bit. The election of 2016 (along with the immense success of J.D. Vance’s <em>Hillbilly Elegy)</em> reminded the nation that people did indeed live in “flyover country." Technically, rural America had always been on the map, but now it was, at least for a moment, on people’s minds. Even if most of the stories seemed a bit scripted and were written by coastal elites who flew in for a few quotes and photographs, many of us in rural communities welcomed the long-overdue attention.</p>
            <p>Perhaps sensing that something had shifted, a small contingent of evangelical authors took up pens to make the most of this uptick in national attention to highlight the needs, potential, and worthiness of small-place ministry. Books like Donnie Grigg’s <em>Small Town Jesus</em> (Everyday Truth, 2016), Brad Roth’s <em>God’s Country</em> (Herald, 2017), Glenn Daman’s <em>The Forgotten Church</em> (Moody, 2018), and Stephen Witmer’s <em>A Big Gospel in Small Places</em> (IVP, 2019) helped to create a new genre of books aimed specifically at small-town pastors. In a few cases, well-known organizations like Wheaton College’s Billy Graham Center joined in on this new emphasis by founding initiatives like the Rural Matters Institute (now called the Rural Church Institute) that addressed the needs and amplified the voices of small-town pastors and rural congregations across the country.</p>
            <p>It was during this time that a group of faculty members and a small-town pastor (me) worked together to start a similar effort at Grove City College in rural, western Pennsylvania. We launched the Project on Rural Ministry (now the Center for Rural Ministry, or CRM) in January of 2019 as an attempt to answer the question: What could a Christian college in a rural community do to help rural pastors flourish?</p>
            <p>In the years since, we have partnered with hundreds of pastors and local lay leaders. We have sent out over a hundred students to invest in small-town churches through internships, our Plowshare program (which helps students build semester-long connections with rural churches outside the typical college-town orbit), and Spring Break service trips. From the start, our primary goals have been to resource pastors; forge connections between college, church, and community; and amplify the voices of rural pastors.</p>
            <p>This last emphasis may seem a bit less important than the first two, but if you think about the number of rural pastors and the extent of their experience in comparison to the places in which that experience can be shared, it’s not hard to see the disconnect we are trying to rectify. Rural and small-town pastors have so much wisdom to offer students and the wider church, but few denominations or conferences seem to be interested in learning from them. Small-town pastors don’t often get featured prominently in denominational publications or on the main stage during conferences. In most cases there is an implicit (but undeniable) sense that it is only the big-church pastors with impressive member roles and platforms who have anything valuable to contribute. Small church pastors are often expected to attend these events in a posture of receiving, as if they have little to give. That’s why part of our work at the CRM has been to highlight and distribute the stories of those ministering and living faithfully in small places through short online blog posts, podcast interviews, short films, and our annual conference.</p>
            <p>In some cases, these stories provide much needed encouragement. When we interviewed Jason Schepp, pastor of Christ United Church in New Castle, Pennsylvania, his account of how meaningful it was to see students devote their Spring Break to serving rural churches was a powerful reminder of how valuable intergenerational ministry can be in rural congregations. Other stories provide fascinating case studies that can inspire other pastors and congregations to develop innovative ministries that meet the needs of the communities they serve. Mark Sentell’s story is one of these.</p>
            <p>Mark is a bi-vocational pastor of Living Faith Church in Fombell, Pennsylvania. From the time Mark planted the church twenty-five years ago, he sensed that the local church needed to pay particular attention to the specific ways it could be a good neighbor in its local context. For Living Faith this has meant developing a flood response ministry and a free Friday lunch. Both of these efforts are uniquely contextualized to the local landscape (the church is located in an area prone to flash floods) and economy (the church is located across the street from a large manufacturing company). More recently, Mark and the members of Living Faith have launched and expanded a car repair ministry called The Garage. At the Garage, Mark and his team love their neighbors by providing car repair at a reduced price (or even for free in some cases). Not only does this meet a real need in rural communities mostly devoid of public transportation, the spiritual fruit has been undeniable. Multiple people have been baptized after encountering Christ through the ministry of The Garage. As Mark notes, the work of The Garage “feels very different than hosting a big event and hoping people will come to our pig roast or something. I like the slow, hands on, every six months we’ll see them for an oil change and can have the next conversation and follow up. I like the speed of investing in something over time.”</p>
            <p>It’s not just testimonials from small place pastors that have potential to change the larger narrative about small places and rural ministry. The feedback we hear from students who participate in partnerships with rural churches through week-long Spring Break service trips, semester-long partnerships with rural churches, and year-long fellowship opportunities with CRM demonstrate how these opportunities help students rethink what a thriving church looks like and where meaningful ministry can happen. A story we recently published on our blog provides an example of how God is using students’ engagement with rural churches to change how they think about success.</p>
            <p>Emily Fox has worked with us at the CRM for the past two years as a writing fellow. In that role, she interviewed many small-town pastors and ministry leaders as well as many college students who had spent time serving in rural churches. The experience changed her. As she told another CRM fellow in an interview this past June, “I thought when I went to college, I needed to get the best education that I possibly could, and I needed to get a big job and move away. I also was just struggling with not feeling like I fit in my community…I felt very isolated, so I just wanted to get away…When I went to college, I went there with the mentality that this was the next step to move on and bury the past.”</p>
            <p>But two years of work with the CRM helped Emily see her hometown with new eyes. When she graduated, instead of moving as far away as she could, she decided to move home and invest in the church she grew up in. She wanted “to use the education that God has provided for me to give back to the place where I found my faith.” Emily was probably as surprised as anyone in this shift in her mindset. But attention changes things. Her work with small-town pastors, congregations, and students serving them helped her learn to love small places like the one she had left.</p>
            <blockquote><p>“Throughout college, that perspective shifted, and the CRM was a big part of that because I was getting to see that yes, there is a lot of hurt that happens in small towns, there's a lot of difficulty… but there's still a lot of good happening in rural communities. Rural communities are hurting and need people who will pour into them and will bring that light of Christ to places that are overlooked. And I was seeing that through the stories that I was writing for the CRM.”</p></blockquote>
            <p>I never get tired of telling stories like these. (If you are interested in hearing more, I invite you to check out the Center for Rural Ministry’s resource page or YouTube channel.) They add important context to the larger conversations about rural America and the rural church. In many cases rural America is painted as either a bucolic haven or, more often, as a dead end for those who can’t get out. The on-the-ground reality, however, seldom lives up to easy stereotypes. To understand any corner of rural America, we need to pay attention to real stories and cultivate real relationships with rural people.</p>
            <p>This is one of the reasons why I have enjoyed both my work as executive director of CRM and as pastor of a local church. Like so many of the pastors we work with, I’ve found investing in rural communities to be good work, but it hasn’t been easy work. Rural communities need more folks to stay, return, or simply choose to show up out of love. I know that God doesn’t call everyone to invest his or her life in a small place, but I also know that our culture makes it hard for those folks God <em>is</em> calling to return to their hometown or minister in a small place, to hear this call clearly over the background noise of a bigger-is-better culture. I wanted to do something to make that call a bit easier to hear.</p>
            <p>My book <em>Strip Mine Jesus</em> is in part the product of my desire to hold up rural stories like those above and help folks all over cultivate ears that might be a bit more receptive to the call of God to go small, if that call comes. The book, part memoir, part diagnostic, part <em>cri de coeur,</em> builds on a decade of rural church literature, but also expands it by addressing not just the pastors who lead the churches but all those who claim the name of Christ and harbor a love for some small or overlooked corner of the world. I hope this book helps individuals like a younger version of myself, who are pulled in multiple directions by a love of a people and a place and a desire to live a meaningful life, listen more attentively and hope more actively right where they are. Because, in the end, we can only love God and neighbor where our feet are.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Strip Mines and Attention</h3>
            <p>Wallace Stegner, an award-winning writer who is also well known as a mentor of Wendell Berry, once wrote that he was “still the person my first fifteen years made me.” I resonate. In many ways, <em>Strip Mine Jesus</em> is my way of working out a theology—of place, leadership, and vocation—that connects with the experiences of my earliest years.</p>
            <p>I only moved once during my childhood and that move was from one side of an unreclaimed strip mine to the other. There’s a whole story about why my parents bought land with an unreclaimed strip mine on it, but the short answer is that there were so many strip mines where I grew up that few folks worried too much about purchasing a bit of earth devoid of top soil and filled with shale and stagnant pools.</p>
            <p>So my parents bought a strip mine.</p>
            <p>My brother and I loved it.</p>
            <p>Over the next two decades my brother and I spent countless hours in the mine riding bikes, picking huckleberries, and ice skating on the pools that formed in the ravines left between spoil piles and high walls when the bulldozers pulled out.</p>
            <p>What we learned over the years, more by intuition and experience than by any explicit instruction, was that attention is powerful. As we attended to this plot of ground through play or through planting pine and spruce saplings in the clay, we came to love these cast-off acres. And like all loves, we found that our love for the strip mine was contagious. As we invited our friends to join us in the mine, many of them came to love the shale-strewn landscape too.</p>
            <p>And it wasn’t only friends who shared these moments with me. I also encountered God in these cast-off acres.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Sehnsucht and Presence</h3>
            <p>Throughout my life there have been moments when I have experienced joy that felt almost uncontainable. Perhaps in these moments I was tasting a bit of the <em>sehnsucht</em> or longing C. S. Lewis describes in his spiritual autobiography <em>Surprised by Joy.</em> For Lewis, moments of fleeting joy that escaped his reach just as he tried to hold on to them became mile markers on his journey with God. They functioned as hints of a distant, seemingly unreachable land.</p>
            <p>Perhaps we all have these longings. I know I always have. It is not hard for me to recognize moments of joyful longing that stand out in my memory. For me, most of these memories are connected to a specific place. For instance, my long-time love of basketball is tied to a few specific memories at local courts. I can remember being overwhelmed by joy the first time I set foot on a basketball court in first grade. I think I had been to a basketball game or two before that, but I had never been able to run on a hardwood floor before. It was love at first sprint. I ran in circles, realizing, even as an eight-year-old, that I was smiling too broadly. The joy—for a reason I couldn’t name or explain—was palpable. A similar thing happened in fourth grade on a different basketball court. It’s no wonder that the basketball court has always been a place I have found God.</p>
            <p>I have experienced a similar sensation of joy in the strip mine. I distinctly remember going for a run in the mine on the afternoon I finished my first year as a high school English teacher. I ran. I smiled. I prayed. I basked in the joy of a task accomplished and the goodness of God amid the clay-smelling ravines and warm spring air. At other times, I can remember the joy of looking on from the top of the high bank as my mom, dad, and brother ice skated below. On that cold day my heart was warm with a sense of contentment that seemed to envelop me.</p>
            <p>Not every talk with God in the mine was joyful. Over the years when melancholy or loneliness—and there has been a good bit of both—crept in, I often made my way to these familiar acres to walk and pray. At other times my strip-mine prayers were intercessory. I can remember standing atop the high wall praying through tears for my friends to know the Lord or, later, for the nearby town of Oil City and our dream of planting a church there.</p>
            <p>It is no wonder that when I first learned about the Christian practice of imaginative prayer in the tradition of Ignatius Loyola and was instructed to locate a place in my memory that was serene and easy to recall, I didn’t need to think very long about where this place might be. I chose a specific corner of the strip mine as a place to regularly sit with Jesus. The practice felt almost intuitive. I had been meeting Jesus on this bit of sacred ground for years.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Blind Spots and Practices That Help Us See</h3>
            <p>As the years passed, I began to see that my experiences in the mine—both with friends and with God—had taught me lessons in the power of attention and the reality of God’s presence. I had learned that love changes the way we see things and that God inhabits casts off places. Both lessons would serve me well as a church planter, pastor, and advocate for small places.</p>
            <p>But neither of these lessons would be easy to remember amid the din of countervailing voices that came at me from a variety of directions. One of the lessons I had to learn—and have to keep learning—is that the idols of our age continue to exert their seemingly subtle and often ubiquitous influence on us in ways that numb our senses. As G. K. Beale notes in his book, <em>We Become What We Worship</em> (IVP Academic, 2008), the Bible is thoroughgoing in its insistence that those who worship idols become like them. When we worship idols, we, like them, may have eyes and ears, but we can’t see or hear correctly.</p>
            <p>If this is true, it should come as no surprise that we find it hard to hear God’s voice or his call on our lives if we have elevated some other thing or desire to the primary place in our hearts. Because we have been created to worship, all worship—whether rightly ordered or idolatrous—comes easy to us. Even though we aren’t often confronted with actual deities that seek to capture our hearts, our culture is full of lesser idols that demand our time, money, and sacrifice. One of the things I try to do in <em>Strip Mine Jesus</em> is showcase how a few of these—celebrity, autonomy, and efficiency—disproportionately hurt small places. I also show how folks can work against the powerful sway of these cultural assumptions by intentionally choosing the goodness of stability, the beauty of community, and the truth of our God-given identities.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Suffering and Hope in Small-Town America</h3>
            <p>Authors don’t always get to have a say in the titles or subtitles a publisher chooses for their books, but I was fortunate enough to be involved in the process. Just like the title, the subtitle, <em>Hope for the Rural Church, Small Towns, and Overlooked Places,</em> matters. Though I include an entire chapter on the reality of suffering in and with small communities, the last word—and the overriding theme—of the book is hope. God has not abandoned small places. His attentive and redemptive presence is just as active in small places as in the most influential global cities.</p>
            <p>This reality means that our work in these places is neither pointless nor forgotten. God can breathe life into the places that folks have left for dead—or left for Nashville or New York. In fact, God already is doing just that. The only question is whether we’ll join him in his good work right where we are.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Charles E. Cotherman is executive director of the Center for Rural Ministry and assistant professor of biblical and theological studies at Grove City College. With permission from the publisher, part of this essay is adapted from his new book, <em>Strip Mine Jesus: Hope for Rural Church, Small Towns, and Overlooked Places</em> (Baker Books, 2026).</p>

