            <p class="rd-essay-n">5 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Take</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Katy Carl</p>

            <p>When they passed the Natural Acts, a nine-hundred-page raft of legislation which V’s husband dismissively referred to as “the law against paper towels,” usually following up with “It’s about damn time, given the rate of deforestation,” V herself had already more or less stopped paying attention to politics. Their daughter was almost a year old now: walking, continually busy, bent on pulling every single item off of any shelf within reach. It was all V could do to keep up with her. In the evenings, V skimmed what articles she could, and though the news of faraway wars and famines always brought stress, the Acts themselves seemed to her to be no cause for alarm.</p>
            <p>The Acts had been drafted and vetted by the Clean Party, whose leadership V was in the unexamined mental habit of trusting implicitly. When acquaintances fretted over tea about the loss of personal autonomy and individual choice, V did her best to set their minds at ease. She said as little as possible about her own habits, because she felt that the laws barely affected her. She herself had already used rags for most cleaning jobs around the house for a long time. She shuttled around as normal: from stove to oven, sink to floor, shower to tub, feeling virtuous and in advance of her time. Of course, there was also the law against disposable diapers, undoubtedly a major inconvenience to many; yet V already used cloth for her baby. She heard rumors that a year’s supply of disposables could be obtained on the black market for $3,000 and wondered who would do such a thing, who would risk prison in the long term for a little less work in the short? Who would even have the extra money for the upfront costs, with the economy the way it was? She heard her fellow mothers begin to complain about lost sleep, extra laundry, stubborn rashes—and, while making sympathetic noises, V quietly wondered too what they were doing wrong; her daughter had none of those problems.</p>
            <p>Because of the laws against paper feminine hygiene products and toilet paper, V found new uses for her diaper sprayer, new pails for soaking, a better enzyme detergent. Then she found that, because of subtle changes in microbial populations in waste water, a new amendment to the Natural Acts outlawed enzyme detergent. Then, because of water rationing, a new amendment outlawed sprayers. Then flushing the toilet more than once every twenty-four hours. Then non-composting toilets. Well, V could afford to install a properly sanitary composting setup, with concrete blocks in the ground beneath the outhouse. Some of her friends ended up with the bucket and sawdust that were technically, under the Acts, illegal. To avoid being arrested for non-compliance with the law, they began to walk over to V’s house twice a day to use the facilities. She offered tea and snacks for those who wanted to stay around afterward; she became a confidante, her home a known safe place, for neighbors who were raising small children.</p>
            <p>V pushed down her feeling of unease at being seen washing her family’s soiled toilet rags, her disgust at the fact that others could smell, in buckets in her own now-useless indoor bathroom, the odor of stained cotton pickling in vinegar. After all, everyone else who couldn’t pay for a cloth service also had to do the same chore now, didn’t they? At least, thank God, it had become an ironclad social rule that you never, ever visited someone’s house without bringing your own cloth items, and you always took these items with you when you left. At least it was only her family’s own laundry V had to do; she wasn’t one of those forced to run an amateur cloth service out of their own homes for extra income. It all began to weigh on V, nevertheless. She began to be tempted by the idea of the $3,000 black-market disposables, to tweak the pie chart of her household budget, to tabulate by how much she would have to reduce her own consumption of resources in order to afford them for her next baby: she was now four months pregnant. But where would she bury them in order not to be found out? She dismissed the idea as impractical, not worth the risk.</p>
            <p>She ordered flannel and cotton fabric in jaunty prints. She built a backyard fire pit, burnt old rags, and sewed new diapers and wipes by the light of the flame, since the electricity no longer stayed on past eight o’clock. In the summer things would be different—no electricity use allowed during sunlight hours, except for six to nine a.m., then again only from nine p.m. to midnight. But V couldn’t wait until the summer. Her daughter would soon need to be toilet trained, and V somehow couldn’t face the task without clean supplies. “Clean” was becoming a more and more relative term, with water and cleaning solutions now rationed and delivered twice yearly, in month’s-supply packages. Friends who already had toddlers were teaching them to use the bathroom twice a day, as most adults did—in the morning and evening—and to squat outside the rest of the time, even on days of snow and ice. Mothers had begun altering their children’s clothing to provide flaps for this purpose. V was a good seamstress; she helped many of her friends do the alterations and made a little independent income that way. She had been a surgeon before the major hospital layoffs in suburban areas. Before long, her skill had helped her family pay off the remaining debt they had accumulated with their local union when credit card systems disappeared—it wasn’t much, given the rate of deflation—and they were saving every bit she brought in.</p>
            <p>She felt so settled that when her husband, also a doctor by training, told her he had found them both new jobs in a clinic in the city, she blurted out, “Why?”</p>
            <p>“Are you joking?” he asked. “Think of having our own brand-new zero-impact apartment. All fitted with the right equipment for a really seamless, healthy life. Meals and laundry and cloth services and waste removal every day, all done by hand, someone else’s hand. Everything we need delivered to the doorstep. No more rags reeking in an uncovered bathtub—”</p>
            <p>“That only happened once,” V interjected. “I couldn’t help that. The buckets needed to be sanitized in the sun.”</p>
            <p>“Well, no more buckets to sanitize, either. No trying to rehydrate cold ration packets from the tiny spigots on those huge water containers. Remember the time K’s daughter was trying to help in the kitchen?”</p>
            <p>“Dropped the jug and shattered it,” V finished the story for him. “Glass everywhere, a seven-inch gash in her arm. I had to stitch it. K whipped her, I think: although God, I can’t believe I said that out loud; don’t tell anyone; I could have heard wrong, it could have been only a joke; I don’t want her to get in any trouble. I’m just speculating, given what I overheard. Maybe they were just yelling at each other. God. I’m sorry I said anything. What do they even do to you for that? I mean, isn’t there also a huge section of new family law in the Natural Acts? Doesn’t it forbid corporal punishment?”</p>
            <p>“No idea; I didn’t really read that much about that part of it. Enforcement never really comes out this far, though. I wouldn’t worry.”</p>
            <p>“Anyway. $1,000 to replace the jug is bad enough. That’s our entire monthly budget.”</p>
            <p>“To say nothing of the human cost. Think of being so angry about something like that that you’d do such a thing to your child. Do you really want to raise children this way, V? Out here like rural farmhands? It’s worse than it was for our great-great-great-grandparents in the Depression. The suburbs are the new prairie. We’ve got to get back to the cities, back to community. A true urban revival. Our grandparents tried, but the technology, the infrastructure, just weren’t in place. Now there’s rooftop farming, composting sewers, biogas-powered energy plants—but only in the cities. Who knows how long before they’re ready to start installing out here. And then, there’s so much resistance against it. No, I want to get back to where we used to be. I want things to be the way they were for us when we’d just graduated from medical school. I want to work in a real hospital with a real operating room. Think of working again, of living down the block from work. Living in a clean place—a really clean place. No weird bathroom fungi, no constant mud and grime. No moss growing in the bottom of your rain barrel, because there <em>is</em> no rain barrel. Cleaning services. School for the children—real school, with teachers. And a place where we can help people, a place for everyone to flourish. A community to serve.”</p>
            <p>“We have a community here,” V said, but mushrooming in her mind were images of her daughter at fourteen, the baby by then twelve—of her two children working side by side in a real classroom, at laptops on standing desks beside a window overlooking the city: a spotless, clear window; an immaculate, dust-free window; a vibrant, exciting city. Already V’s imagination was giving her current children more little siblings: a third- and a first-grader with smooth cheeks and bright eyes. In her mind’s eye they were painting pictures in a warm apricot-colored studio with wood floors; they were pouring water on classroom window boxes of rosemary, parsley, mint, lemon balm. V saw herself wearing clean teal scrubs, free of the microparticles of grit that embedded themselves in all fabric now and lurked in the corners of the broken laundry machine she used as a washtub. She saw herself treating patients again, healing bodies: a priestess devoted to reconsecrating ruined temples of the spirit, to making broken vessels of the sacred whole. She had signed the contract before she had time to give it another thought.</p>
            <p>In the city, in the zero-impact apartment, V’s second child was born—another girl—delivered by midwives who brought their own sterile equipment, scrubbed away all traces of blood and amniotic fluid down a drain in the middle of the main room’s pristine floor, cleaned V and the baby with warm lavender-scented cloths, and left a soy candle burning on the windowsill by the nursing chair when they departed. Each day the meal service brought portions of pulse-and-rice bowls, roasted root vegetable soups, bran breads, spiced tofu, greens, whole fruits. Because V was breastfeeding, she received an egg-and-dairy allowance. Because she and her husband both worked, they were brought coffee, black and steaming, in paper cups each morning. Soon V began pumping milk for the baby; though the damp silicone shields chafed her breasts red, she smeared them with synthetic lanolin and kept on, preferring the pain over resorting to even the best formulas: yes, the manufacturers could now replicate every element of human milk but the immune factors; still, V wanted her baby to have even those if possible. She stored milk in a small cube-shaped freezer, another social concession their apartment uniquely offered to her early motherhood: most apartments contained no appliances; none had kitchens.</p>
            <p>V never anticipated that this might present a problem until the morning her elder daughter, A, then two and a half, woke up and pronounced, “Bird day.”</p>
            <p>“What?”</p>
            <p>“Nonny bird day.”</p>
            <p>“Nonny is a baby,” V said through a yawn. “A child, a girl. A little human. Like you. She isn’t a bird, silly.”</p>
            <p>“Bird day,” A reiterated.</p>
            <p>“Oh, she’s a bird today?” Flights of fancy: mustn’t discourage them.</p>
            <p>“No, BIRD day. Nonny need take.”</p>
            <p>“Take . . . what? Take her where?”</p>
            <p>“Not take take. Nonny DET take. Pink tandles.”</p>
            <p>“Get . . . Candles?”</p>
            <p>“Bird day take. Nonny EAT it.”</p>
            <p>“Oh. Birthday cake.”</p>
            <p>“Yes. She eat take.”</p>
            <p>“Right, Nonny will be one year old soon. Well, let’s go see what the meal servers brought us today.” V opened the door. In the hall, one small credenza stood by each apartment entry; this was where meals were left. Stacked on V’s family’s credenza were three meal boxes per person: six about the size and shape of a thick trade hardback novel, an object V hadn’t handled since her childhood, and six more about a third of that size. V dutifully opened and perused these with A.</p>
            <p>“Mommy’s got eggs and veggies for breakfast, soup and salad for lunch, lentils and rice for dinner. There’s Daddy’s tempeh, mushroom sandwiches, and rice bowl just like mine, only a little bigger. You and Nonny have eggs and cheese, fruit and sunflower butter sandwiches, and rice and veggies. Yum, sweetie, hooray,” she chirped.</p>
            <p>A frowned. “It not Nonny’s bird day?”</p>
            <p>“Honestly, sweetie, I don’t really remember which day she was born. I would have to look it up.”</p>
            <p>“Nonny’s bird day tomorrow, mama? Take tomorrow?”</p>
            <p>“Maybe, sweetie. We’ll just have to see what happens.”</p>
            <p>They gathered the meal boxes and folded clothing from the credenza; they ate and dressed. Then V bundled the girls into the elevator, into the bicycle trailer, and down the block to the care center, as she did before any shift that began when her husband was already working. He would pick the girls up later. Sometimes V wondered whether it was disorienting to the girls that sometimes their “morning” began at five a.m., sometimes at ten; that sometimes their mother had them, sometimes their father. There had been signs of strain when they first moved and when V went back to work after Nonny was born, but now they seemed to have reached a plateau. Sometimes V felt a pang of regret as she handed the soft, microfleece-swathed bundle that was Nonny’s body through the little opening above the safety gate, into the arms of a smiling young woman who looked remarkably like V herself ten years ago. V remembered the snug warmth of A’s infant-round torso against her own, the hungry little mouth at her breast, the starfish-splayed hand seeking reassurance from V’s hair, her skin, her clothes. By contrast, V’s physical memory of Nonny was more like her memory of the exterior of one of the clean smooth meal parcels that were dropped off on the credenza each morning and picked up each evening. A patient baby, Nonny never complained about being popped back into the bike trailer, already bathed and pajamaed and ready to place in bed, where she lay without complaint blowing bubbles at the ceiling until she fell asleep. When V tried to rest her cheek against the little one’s, where A had once nestled up to her, Nonny rolled away and buried her face in the pillow.</p>
            <p>They all slept in the same bed. When they woke, A ran straight to the credenza, grabbed the meal boxes, and set them on the counter by the monitor. By the time V had stumbled her way to the main room, A had opened each box and methodically taken it apart with little fingers, raking rows in mounds of quinoa, spilling large chunks of vegetable, mashing beans. All of the tofu lay on the floor, gathering microparticles of dirt. V’s fried egg had broken, leaking yellow yolk into the rug.</p>
            <p>V lacked the energy to yell. “Let’s clean this up,” she said to A, scooping food back into the boxes with her own fingers. A joined in the task.</p>
            <p>“What did I tell you,” V droned. “Let Mommy or Daddy handle the boxes. We aren’t allowed extra. If we spill, it is all gone. That is all the food we get until tomorrow.”</p>
            <p>“I shek for take,” A explained patiently, as if V were very small and couldn’t be expected to understand.</p>
            <p>“You checked for cake,” V echoed hollowly. “Honey, I don’t think they’re going to send cake. I don’t think it’s even Nonny’s birthday. I still haven’t looked up what day it’s supposed to be.”</p>
            <p>“Dey send take,” A asserted, nodding. “Soon.”</p>
            <p>But no cake was sent. Still, A went on rifling the food containers despite repeated explanations and incentives to do otherwise, despite repeated warnings, time-outs, consequences, lost privileges: everything in V’s toolbox, plus plenty she made up on the fly. A often spilled and frequently ate, sometimes more than her share of the food, while she searched. At times hardly anything was left for the rest of the family. V saved what she could from the floor, which was only possible because the cleaners came every day while the family was out, and no one wore shoes during the brief time they were at home anyway. Even with the salvaged food, she often had to stint herself and her husband so that the children would have enough to last them the day. The one time she tried refusing to send any food with A to the care center, on the grounds that A had already eaten her entire daily ration plus most of her sister’s, the worker at the gate refused to accept this. She said that A wouldn’t be able to stay at the center without proper meals, and in order to make it to her shift at all V had to redistribute and repack the containers there on the sidewalk, giving the children almost every bite she had planned to take with her to work. V was late that day and made several lightheaded mistakes on the job: minor ones, but she ended up being berated by a supervisor and, bicycling home in tears, she nearly collided with one of the only vehicles still allowed on the road (a supply delivery truck).</p>
            <p>V began to have nightmares. She woke up gasping, again and again, certain that she’d amputated a wrong limb or left a scalpel inside an abdomen. She began to avoid going to sleep, which naturally made her woozier and more prone to error. Then came the day when A cried inconsolably, all morning, throughout breakfast, throughout dressing, down the elevator, into the bike trailer, along the sidewalk, through the gate.</p>
            <p>“Take,” A wailed, again and again, until the word had the force of a stick striking V between the shoulder blades. “Ta-a-a-a-ke.”</p>
            <p>“She doesn’t want me to take her?” frowned the pretty care center worker, pressing A.’s sobbing, heaving body to her pink smock.</p>
            <p>V explained. “She wants cake for her little sister’s birthday. She doesn’t know she has a birthday, herself, but she seems to remember Nonny being born.”</p>
            <p>“Huh.” The carer’s frown deepened. According to the theory of child development she’d been taught, it didn’t seem to make sense to her that such a young toddler would have such a clear memory. “Who taught her about birthdays?”</p>
            <p>“I’m sure it wasn’t me. Maybe one of the other children said something.” V doubted it. She pictured instead an old book lying around in the care center—perhaps it was the same one V herself remembered from childhood, in which a little girl about A’s age lived through a whole peaceful year of seasons and holidays and, at the end of the book, her own pink-frosted birthday—but to mention such a possibility seemed accusatory.</p>
            <p>The carer shook her head. “I doubt it, but I’ll ask around. Some families still celebrate, you know, but it’s pretty inappropriate, if you ask me, given the circumstances. Just insensitive.”</p>
            <p>The pitch of the carer’s voice shifted lower as she spoke. She was alluding to the fact, not mentioned in front of children, that only about a quarter of worldwide pregnancies in the past twenty years had made it to term. Researchers hadn’t identified the cause of the out-of-control miscarriage rate, but public opinion took it for granted that environmental catastrophe lay at the heart of this problem as of so many others. There were darker rumors, though widely dismissed as conspiracy theory, about selective water-supply contamination with low-level progestins as a population control measure. A friend of V’s—a fellow doctor, a medical-school classmate—had made her career as a science writer debunking the idea that such a scheme was even plausible, let alone possible to implement. So V kept her wondering to herself. Her family, like most families now, had stopped marking or celebrating birthdays, to avoid reminding friends and neighbors of wished-for events that hadn’t taken place.</p>
            <p>At work, during lunch, V asked her husband whether he had mentioned birthdays to A. “Reminiscing, telling her about your childhood? Nothing like that?”</p>
            <p>“No,” he said. “I wish we could, but it would be kind of awful, you know? Like people who used to eat shark-fin soup while the rest of the city starved. We’re already off-the-charts privileged because both of our girls made it. Celebrating on top of that would be like rubbing it in everyone’s faces.”</p>
            <p>V privately disagreed—after all, who, outside their little apartment, would see the glow of candlelight on Nonny’s moonlike face, who could know or care? Who might even have the opportunity, let alone the right, to mind?—but she let it pass. Her husband kept talking.</p>
            <p>“You can request cake, I’ve heard, but it’s not a good idea. The people who work in the kitchens have lost children, too, of course. I’ve heard stories where people put in the request form—it’s three months in advance, just like any other dietary change request—and on the day, they got nothing to eat but boxes of compost. Not exactly a subtle message.”</p>
            <p>V took her last bite of curried lentils. As usual, even on days when A didn’t ransack and pillage, there was about ten percent too little. Her stomach rumbled. “Still, certainly it isn’t always like that. I might try. I’d take any chance at this point if it would calm Anastasia’s behavior.”</p>
            <p>“Tell me about it. The mess, the waste. And I’m always hungry.”</p>
            <p>So after her shift, before picking up the children, V biked fifteen blocks to the nearest communal kitchen. At the front window, where one worker sat to manage the office, V filled out and turned in the required form. The worker, a dark-haired young woman in a sleek gray dress, murmured a perfunctory “thanks” without looking up from her tablet as V left.</p>
            <p>Riding to the care center through the blue dusk, as light after soft white light went on in apartment windows above darkened storefronts, V wondered what it must look like inside the communal kitchens themselves. Journalists coveted a glimpse, suspecting that the kitchens harbored one of those lingering situations of underground oppression left over from the days before the Natural Acts were passed. V expected the journalists were right as far as that went, she really believed this way was much more socially just, but she still couldn’t shake a mental image of smooth white marble counters, high sunny windows, warm red brick walls, a bank of stand freezers like icebergs; another image of rows of hands at work, kneading dough, rolling crusts, mixing and stirring and dipping, in clouds of steam scented with cardamom and cinnamon and clove. V pictured this to herself all the way home, as she absently pedaled and just as absently answered A’s chatty questions. All throughout their ten p.m. family dinner, V was silent as she remembered the warm clean kitchen of the neighbor who had invited V over to learn to bake bread the summer she was twelve: the grit of cracked wheat, the glue of molasses, between her fingers; the green light filtering through the leaves of the philodendron hanging in the breakfast nook’s high bay window; the smell, the smell. V slept and dreamed all night of that summer kitchen, of teaching her girls to cook in a house of their own in the countryside.</p>
            <p>V’s husband had to work a double shift that night, so V stayed alone with the girls in the apartment. She had done an early-morning surgery a few days ago and was still tired. She slept too soundly to notice when A woke before dawn, collected the meal boxes, emptied them out on the rug, ate what she wanted, blended and ground the remainder of the family’s food into an indistinguishable mush with her hands, and then began to sob.</p>
            <p>V didn’t rouse until she heard A’s now-familiar cry: “No take. No ta-a-a-ke.”</p>
            <p>V dragged herself from bedroom to main room, looked, saw, and slapped A’s face. She had never done such a thing before. Shocked by the force of her own anger, she slapped A again, then collapsed, crying, as A stared without comprehension. One more moment passed, and now they both cried together, holding each other tightly. A had fallen straight forward into V’s arms, smearing her with a tomato marsala sauce that, now on their hands and sleeves and now all over the front of V’s pajamas, looked like blood.</p>
            <p>“I’m sorry, I’m sorry,” repeated V over and over again, stroking and kissing her daughter’s hair, but she never figured out whether it was the slap or the apology that prompted A to narrate the incident to her carers, which A must have done, because how else would anyone have known? V would never have texted her husband about the incident, as the texts would surely have been picked up by Homeland Security agents. Nor, for similar reasons, would she have been so foolish as to confide in anyone. V only cleaned up the mess that morning and delivered her daughters to the center. When V came after her shift to pick them up, as usual, tears sprang into the eyes of the family’s favorite carer as soon as she saw her.</p>
            <p>“I already sent a message to your husband,” was all the carer said. “He’ll be here to take the girls soon.”</p>
            <p>V began to ask what the younger woman meant. Two men stepped out of the shadows at either side of the open, lighted passageway. They each took one of V’s elbows and cuffed her quickly. Too surprised to scream, she took the slip of receipt-type paper, folded neatly in half once, that the left-hand man handed to her. The men led her toward a car (when was the last time V had seen a real car?) and seated her inside, buckling her seatbelt for her as you might do for a child. They settled themselves along the front bench, pressed the starter, and let the car take them away.</p>
            <p>The overhead light remained on when the door shut, so that V could read:</p>
            <blockquote><p>WHEREAS children are our society’s most precious resource . . . to strike a child a felony . . . punishable by 30 years minimum penitentiary service . . . when verified by a witness of whatever age, including the victim, no appeal shall be granted. <em>(Natural Acts,</em> 781.41.§4)</p></blockquote>
            <p>These were the last words V ever read before the world became to her nothing but a steely ray of light from a slit in a high barred window, a cast-iron cooking pan steaming in front of her—a batch in, a batch out, a batch in, a batch out, over and over—fourteen hours a day, an aluminum tray of beans and bread before lights-out, hands constantly sticky and smelling slightly of molasses, and at night the dreams: a child seated on her lap, a little girl with soft fragrant hair and a tiny, warm peach of a cheek pressed against hers. Night after night, the child raised a small hand to V’s mouth and fed her a sliver of angel food cake that dissolved on V’s tongue, slipped down her throat, and was gone.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Katy Carl is editor at Word on Fire Luminor, a literary imprint for serious readers of any faith or none, and writer in residence at the University of St. Thomas—Houston. Her work has appeared in <em>America</em>, <em>Angelus</em>, <em>Church Life Journal</em>, <em>Colosseum</em>, <em>Ekstasis</em>, <em>Evangelization &amp; Culture</em>, <em>Fare Forward</em>, <em>Literary Matters</em>, <em>Mere Orthodoxy</em>, <em>National Review</em>, <em>Public Discourse</em>, <em>Windhover</em>, <em>World Literature Today</em>, and others.</p>

