          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 5 ====== -->
          <section id="evangelicals-on-trial" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">6 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Evangelicals on Trial</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Nadya Williams</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Kristin Kobes Du Mez. <em>Live Laugh Love: The Secret History of White Christian Women and the World They Made</em>. Liveright, 2026. $31.99. 432 pp.</p>
            <p class="essay-heading">Bonnie Kristian. <em>In Defense of Evangelicalism: A Response to Its Cultured Despisers</em>. Forum Books, 2026. $27.00. 224 pp.</p>
            <p>There was a righteous man once, a long time ago. He lived in the land of Uz, if you must know. He was a righteous man who loved God, and God blessed him greatly for his righteousness—he had many children; he was exceedingly wealthy, if one measured wealth in sheep, which in his world, one did; he had a happy marriage, good health, and good friends. He was living an abundant and flourishing life, while also being generous to others. Until one day, mysteriously, God took it all away.</p>
            <p>His children tragically perished when a roof caved in. His wealth vanished into thin air. His very body became covered with boils and oozing sores. His wife began reproaching him, encouraging him to curse God and die already. Couldn’t he see that his life was no longer worth living? His friends accused him of being a hypocrite and sinner, who clearly deserved all this and needed to repent to God of his sins—or, alternatively, curse God and die already. The man refused all such advice, though. He continued to worship God, and while he refused to blame God for his extraordinary suffering, he also refused to say he deserved all this as recompense for his sins. At the end, God restored to him his previous health and wealth, blessed him with ten new children, and gave him a long life to enjoy all this too.</p>
            <p>You have probably recognized this story by now. The righteous man in question is, of course, Job. The protagonist of one of the wisdom books of the Bible, Job suffered the extraordinary evils that became his lot overnight because of a test, a conversation between God and Satan over whether anyone could truly and genuinely love God when beset by every evil imaginable. Job, it appears, largely passed this difficult test—although towards the end, even while he continued to worship God, he had some questions too. But how might we think of a real-life Job today, someone who suffers incredible hardships, yet still turns to God as his or her main source of comfort?</p>
            <p>I thought about this question as I was reading Kristin Kobez Du Mez's new book, <em>Live Laugh Love: The Secret History of White Christian Women and the World They Made.</em> Du Mez envisions this book as the natural sequel to her 2020 <em>New York Times</em> bestseller, <em>Jesus and John Wayne: How White Evangelicals Corrupted a Faith and Fractured a Nation,</em> an indicting account of American evangelicalism as defined by the worship of excessive violence and patriarchy above all else. But while the former book dealt with the failings of white evangelical men, this new book turns to the no less considerable failings of white evangelical women—and some other Christian women who are evangelical-adjacent enough to be guilty of the same sins. Chief among them is what some critics of evangelicalism, Du Mez foremost among them, see as the sin of Job—namely, continuing to worship God even in times of suffering. The fruit of such worship, Du Mez contends, is toxic positivity that infects others, wretched enough to follow the Job-like models.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">The Sin of Job</h3>
            <p>“Hannah’s life was anything but happy by any conventional measure,” writes Du Mez in opening her book with the story of Hannah Whitall Smith, the author of the 1875 bestseller <em>The Christian’s Secret of a Happy Life.</em> Just what might be this secret for someone who (as Whitall Smith did) lost four children to illnesses and struggled to support a husband beset by mental health troubles, even as her own health was failing? For Whitall Smith, as for Job, the secret was her faith. But was this a good thing, Du Mez asks?</p>
            <blockquote><p>“Hannah’s conversion to holiness Christianity had freed her from a life of anxious struggle, at least for a time. For many who followed in her footsteps, it would do the opposite. In the hands of others, Hannah’s emphasis on submission to God would be recast for women as submission to men. Her attempts to find God in the brokenness of her own life would lead others to expect God to ‘supernaturally remove all difficulty and pain’ from theirs.”</p></blockquote>
            <p>The women whose lives Du Mez documents in this book have suffered much. Elisabeth Elliot lost her first husband, the missionary Jim Elliot, when he and four other missionaries were killed by the Waorani, the unreached tribe they were trying to convert. Elliot “cabled her parents: ‘The Lord keeps me in peace,’” before moving into the Waorani village to continue Jim’s ministry. Du Mez is not impressed with this self-sacrifice and devotion to God either, painting Elliot as both victim of the more assertive men in her life and a calculating smooth operator trying to launch a successful publishing career by capitalizing on her own suffering.</p>
            <p>And then there are the miserable housewives-turned-bestselling authors of the 1970s, like Helen Andelin, the author of <em>Fascinating Womanhood,</em> and Marabel Morgan, the author of <em>The Total Woman.</em> Each woman took her domestic struggles of trying to build a happy marriage and turned them into a lucrative platform of classes and books. Morgan obviously plagiarized Andelin, but the incredible popularity of both suggests something else that is no less obvious—that there was obvious demand for the product they were selling, their own version of the secret of Job. We can (and should) note that the books’ Christian content did not extend much beyond sprinkling Bible verses throughout, and some of these women certainly turned their suffering into opportunistic businesses. Still, their popularity among Christian women just as debates over feminism and the Equal Rights Amendment were reaching a fever pitch is telling.</p>
            <p>Continuing chronologically into the present, in the final part of the book Du Mez considers women like Beth Moore, who overcame a childhood beset by trauma and sexual abuse by her own father to become arguably the most popular women’s Bible teacher today. And there is Ann Voscamp, an exhausted mother of six small children when she first started writing her blog, which led to her bestselling book <em>One Thousand Gifts: A Dare to Live Fully Right Where You Are.</em> Like Hannah Whitall Smith, it seems, these twenty-first-century women embraced the same answer that Job once adopted in his own darkest days—that God’s sovereignty and love hold the believer fast even in the midst of suffering. The popularity of their books shows that this answer still appeals to many Christian women today.</p>
            <p>It is this answer that Du Mez questions in this book, as the sins of Job become the sins of all white Christian women who, through their firm belief in God amidst suffering and trauma did not choose to overturn their oppressors (meaning, obviously, the men in their lives and the society at large) but chose to trust God instead. Du Mez’s argument in this book is that this faith in God in the midst of suffering made these women and all who have followed them complicit in everything that has (in her view) politically gone astray in America right now, from the election and re-election of Trump to the overthrow of <em>Roe</em> and more. “Patriarchy, White Supremacy, &amp; Violent Religious Nationalism are one hell of an unholy trinity,” wrote Rachel Held Evans in 2018, and with this quotation from Evans, Du Mez concludes her own book.</p>
            <p>Du Mez has always been an exquisite historian and researcher, and the stories she tells in this book show that she continues to be the master of the research craft. But history is not an exact science, reducible to formulas. In his 1988 doorstopper, <em>That Noble Dream: The “Objectivity Question” and the American Historical Profession,</em> historian Peter Novick examined the elusive “noble dream” of modern historians—the naïve belief that history too is an objective science, much like chemistry. Unlike chemists, whose laboratory experiments must be replicable, when professional historians set out to tell a story, they choose what aspects of a story to emphasize and highlight, which ones to leave out, and what conclusions to draw as a result. This means that someone else writing a history of white evangelical women in America could tell an entirely different story from Du Mez.</p>
            <p>Indeed, Du Mez’s narrative is the result of a common myopia in the American secular academic profession today: It relies on the conviction that everything in history runs on power. In the case of American evangelical history, then, all people are either victims of a power struggle or the perpetrators of power injustices. The few, the proud, the true historians, of course, are the prophetic voices trying to disrupt this dynamic by “speaking truth to power.”</p>
            <p>And yet, could it be that not every single thing that Du Mez thinks is political is, in fact, political? Was the biblical Job making a political statement against some kind of oppression when he chose to continue praising God amidst the evils that beset him on every side? What if the average Christian who suffers earnestly does want to find that “the joy of the Lord is my strength” (Nehemiah 8:10)? Does this make that person an idiot or evil? Or could it be that the majority of evangelicals are sincere faithful people who are good for America, just as the early Church Fathers argued that Christians were good for the Roman Empire? These are the questions Bonnie Kristian, Deputy Editor at <em>Christianity Today,</em> takes on in her new book, which reads effectively as a response to Du Mez’s charges against evangelicals.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Redeeming Evangelicals</h3>
            <p>Bonnie Kristian is not a historian. She is, rather, a journalist, and in her book, she relies on this strength to have conversations with real evangelicals across America—including college professors, spa owners, and a pastor’s wife of a Dallas-area “evangelical, Spanish-language congregation composed almost entirely of first-and second-generation immigrants. Most of those who aren’t U.S. citizens are in the country illegally, and most of those who are citizens voted for Donald Trump. By the time we sat down for an interview after the service, the building was emptying out following a lunch of chicken tortas.”</p>
            <p>Real people, Kristian wants us to see through stories like this one, defy simplistic stereotypes to which evangelicalism’s critics repeatedly turn. Could it be that evangelicals, many of whom are the racial minorities Du Mez and her ilk paint as the victims of white evangelicalism, are actually believers themselves, and simply want to live faithful lives in this country?</p>
            <p>In interview after interview, Kristian found significant racial diversity and firm sincerity of belief among evangelicals in the pews. She also found a desire to be allowed to practice these beliefs in the face of lawsuits from, for example, LGBTQ activist groups. “We all live and work in a country with ambient resentment toward public expressions of serious, life-directing religion,” Kristian remarks. “American norms on premarital sex, adultery, divorce, and gay marriage have shifted with remarkable speed. On trans issues, the change has been even faster, if not quite as complete.” She brings up the 2023 California law, according to which “you can no longer serve as a foster parent if you’re not willing to provide an ‘affirming home’ to a child with an LGBTQ identity.” In other words, Christians who hold traditional views on sexuality are no longer eligible to be foster parents in the entire state of California. Who is really on a power trip here, we may ask? Not evangelicals.</p>
            <p>Kristian is well familiar with other book-length critiques of evangelicals on a power trip. She devotes an entire chapter early in her book to responding to the work of Kristin Du Mez and Beth Allison Barr. Later on, she also notes another book in the genre—journalist Katherine Stewart’s <em>The Power Worshippers: Inside the Dangerous Rise of Religious Nationalism.</em> Kristian quotes at length Stewart’s alarm over an organization that is particularly to blame for the dismantling of democracy in the world and its replacement by Christian nationalist right-wing authoritarians.</p>
            <p>Except, the organization in question turns out to be “The Gospel Coalition, a theologically conservative but temperamentally and politically moderate evangelical network. On their homepage as I write these words,” notes Kristian, “are articles and podcasts about overcoming perfectionism, being gracious to your spouse, taking better care of the environment, attacking historic justifications for slavery, and encouraging pastors and laypeople to spend more time reading the Bible.”</p>
            <p>There is an irony, I thought, in reading a journalist who advances these arguments. It is supposed to be the job of professional historians to combat simplistic arguments and to complicate them by suggesting nuance. But in the case of American evangelicalism, it seems that most recently, some historians of evangelicalism have become the most ardent “cultured despisers” of the book’s subtitle. This posture shows a crisis of faith among those who adopt it. But it doesn’t have to be that way. Kristian reminds us that the vast majority of American evangelicals are not deranged patriarchal maniacs who would like to orchestrate a global takeover and install Doug Wilson as their flame-throwing wife-spanking leader. Just because the craziest voices receive the most oxygen in the news and on social media does not mean that they really are mainstream in evangelical culture. Indeed, perhaps the best way to fall in love with American evangelicalism is to simply find a healthy local church, become a member, and get to know fellow Christ-lovers in the pews.</p>
            <p>I have written at length elsewhere about the wonderful local churches to which my family has belonged for many years in Georgia, and over the past three years in Ohio. While ours has not been the suffering of Job, we have experienced our own small tragedies over the years, for such is mankind’s lot under the sun. And repeatedly, it was the care of the local church that upheld us. We cannot imagine our lives without our local church, without these people with whom we share our joys, or sorrows, and our innermost prayers. Are they (and we) still deeply flawed sinners in need of God’s saving grace? Of course. But with our imperfect hearts, minds, souls, and strength we continue to pursue the commandment to love the Lord our God and each other.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Nadya Williams is managing editor at Mere Orthodoxy and the host of Christians Reading Classics podcast. She is a senior fellow in the Ashbrook Center at Ashland University, where she also directs the MFA in creative writing. She is the author of three books, the latest of which is <em>Christians Reading Classics</em>. Her next book, <em>The Literate Soul: What Four Thousand Years of Reading Books Teaches Us about Loving God</em>, is coming in 2027 from Brazos Press.</p>

    <a class="rd-next" href="#a-conclusion-in-search"><span class="rd-next-label">Next essay</span><span class="rd-next-title">A Conclusion in Search of Evidence</span></a>
