          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 7 ====== -->
          <section id="a-forrest-gump-view" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">8 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A Forrest Gump View of Parental Rights</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Joseph K. Griffith II</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Julia Bowes. <em>Every Man's Home a Castle</em>. Princeton University Press: 2026. $29.95, 264 pp.</p>
            <p>In the iconic film <em>Forrest Gump</em> (1994), the titular hero is a man with a good heart but a below-average intelligence. He becomes an unintended key player in the history of the latter half of the twentieth century, as he teaches Elvis how to dance, accidentally helps desegregate the University of Alabama, and opens the door to Chinese-American relations through his “ping-pong diplomacy.” The result is an entertaining mish-mash of a tale that only makes sense in a good film.</p>
            <p>I was reminded of <em>Forrest Gump</em> while reading <em>Every Man’s Home a Castle.</em> In this new history of parental rights, gender studies professor Julia Bowes attempts to “recast[] the origins of modern conservatism” as an eclectic, reactionary, insular, and even wacky movement in defense of white patriarchy, united under the banner of parental rights. She focuses on “anti-statist” opposition to three humanitarian movements in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries: public schools, vaccines, and child-labor laws. Like the story of Forrest Gump, Bowes is right on many of the details she covers. Still, the book falls short on a number of counts—mostly in its overarching narrative about the right of parents to direct their children’s education.</p>
            <p>In other words, the problem with the book is not the individual episodes it recounts but its conclusion that a “widely held worldview” of the period under consideration was “that the white male-headed family was its own form of government that ought to be beyond the reach of the state.” The real story, however, is much more complicated than Bowes imagines.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Three Forms of Anti-Statism</h3>
            <p>Bowes begins with public schools. In the mid-nineteenth century, Republicans advanced compulsory school attendance laws in order to assimilate European immigrants, lift up freed slaves, and educate all children for the duties of republican self-government. Donning Horace Mann’s mantle, anti-clerical Protestants led the charge for compulsory school laws after the Civil War, their rhetoric tinged with more than a little anti-Catholicism and governmental paternalism. As then-Congressman James A. Garfield declared in 1866, with three-hundred thousand immigrants “annually thrown upon our shores” and four million freed slaves recently added as citizens, the question of education “demand[s] the best efforts of statesmanship.”</p>
            <p>Though sometimes proceeding in fits and starts, every state passed a compulsory schooling law by the early twentieth century, and state supreme courts consistently upheld them as constitutional. According to Bowes, this meant that, “by the late nineteenth century, a widespread legal consensus had formed that the state was the ultimate parent of the child.”</p>
            <p>In response, a loose coalition of Catholics, Social Darwinists, and Southern aristocrats opposed compulsory school attendance laws. Strange bedfellows, they latched onto the language of parental rights—but for very different reasons. Catholics argued that “parental rights precede State rights.” In California, Zachariah Montgomery made “a lot of noise” defending the “autonomy” (Bowe’s term) of the family. Meanwhile in Virginia, Robert Lewis Dabney and J. William Jones opposed the federal government’s imposition of “negro suffrage and the public school” as a threat to the (white) social order of the South. Eventually, the Democratic Party seized the opportunity to coalesce its “disparate and often fragile coalition,” declaring in its 1892 platform its opposition “to State interference with parental rights and rights of conscience in the education of children.”</p>
            <p>According to Bowes, the Supreme Court then “broke new ground” in its landmark decisions of <em>Meyer v. Nebraska</em> (1923) and <em>Pierce v. Society of Sisters</em> (1925). Unveiling “an alternative jurisprudence,” the Court embraced parental rights as “the mark and measure of the free rights of men” and struck down a Nebraska law prohibiting instruction in foreign languages and an Oregon law effectively outlawing private schools. “Notwithstanding the profound scriptural differences” between them, Lutherans and Catholics met on the common ground of patriarchy and led the charge, Bowes contends. As she summarizes it, their lawyers “piec[ed] together a relatively obscure list of precedent” to make their case—and won.</p>
            <p>Second, Bowes recounts the rise of the anti-vaccination movement. In the early-twentieth century, the heyday of eugenics in America, an army of “biological engineers” seeking to “advance our civilization” by curing feeble-minded children of “vicious, drunken, or imbecilic ancestry” attempted to mandate vaccinations in public schools. But for Bowes, it seems, the real villains are the leaders of a reactionary “anti-vaccination and alternative health movement,” including the American Anti-Vaccination League and the National League for Medical Freedom. While many of their foot soldiers were women who made “sentimental” arguments about maternal love, the leaders advocated for “the right of independent <em>men</em> to make decisions on behalf of their dependent kin.”</p>
            <p>Third, Bowes explores how a conservative lobbying group, the Sentinels of the Republic, helped stop the passage of a Federal Child Labor Amendment in the 1920s and 1930s. For example, citing the Court’s opinion in <em>Pierce,</em> Columbia University President Nicholas Murray Butler opposed the amendment because it would initiate a “complete revolution in our family and social life.”</p>
            <p>Oddly, Bowes does not address the Supreme Court’s 1905 decision upholding a Massachusetts law mandating vaccines, or the court’s 1944 opinion rejecting the argument that parental rights trump the government’s authority to prevent child labor.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Racism and Patriarchy around Every Turn</h3>
            <p>The chapters on education are full of interesting individual anecdotes, but there are problems with the book’s understanding of history, constitutional law, and organizing theory.</p>
            <p>For one, Bowes offers a reductive account of religious parents and overstates Catholic commitment to the “absolute” authority of parents over the education of their children. Bowes characterizes Catholic and Lutheran support for parental rights as motivated, at bottom, by “a strict hierarchal and patriarchal conception of the family” rather than by the desire to protect the intimate and loving care of parents for their children. Bowes quotes Pope Leo XIII’s 1891 encyclical <em>Rerum Novarum—“paternal</em> authority can neither be abolished nor absorbed by the state”—to demonstrate “the staid opposition of Catholics to public education.” Certainly, Pope Leo warns against the impulse to violate “natural justice” by destroying “the structure of the home,” but he does not oppose public schools or public authority in general. Instead, in that same paragraph, he admits that public authorities must intervene in cases of “grave disturbance of mutual rights,” “since each family is a part of the commonwealth.”</p>
            <p>And there’s a timeline problem. Bowes claims that parental rights were effectively code for the supremacy of the white patriarchy but then proceeds to mostly ignore the first parental rights advocates in American history: the abolitionists. In the antebellum years, northern abolitionists defended the rights of enslaved fathers <em>and mothers.</em> According to Herbert Gutman, the “classic indictment of slavery” was the enslaver’s power to separate families, violating the rights of mothers, fathers, and children. One abolitionist wrote that slavery annihilates “the parental relation” because “the authority of a slave father or mother over their child is not recognized by the slaveholder in the slightest degree.” If the enslaved father, mother, and child are all “the property of the slaveholder,” then enslaved parents have no “right” to direct their child “contrary to the will of him who claims to own them,” he wrote.</p>
            <p>Similarly, in her attempts to show that support for parental rights around the turn of the twentieth century helps us better understand <em>modern</em> conservatism, Bowes would have made a more persuasive argument, had she extended her analysis past the 1930s. For example, many white southerners used the rhetoric of parental rights to oppose desegregation in the wake of <em>Brown v. Board of Education.</em> In 1956, nearly one-hundred politicians signed onto Senator Strom Thurmond’s “Southern Manifesto,” claiming that “parents should not be deprived by government of the right to direct the lives and education of their own children.” Bowes explores white southerners’ opposition to public education during Reconstruction but not during the massive resistance movement, leaving the link between debates about compulsory education in the nineteenth century and the modern conservative movement weaker than it could be.</p>
            <p><em>Every Man’s Home a Castle</em> is wrong on the question of constitutional law as well. Bowes contends that, in <em>Meyer</em> and <em>Pierce,</em> the Supreme Court overturned a “half century” of state-court jurisprudence that had established the state’s power over parents as “expansive and seemingly limitless.” It’s a familiar argument about the retrenchment of an archaic view of the family at the end of the progressive era. But in <em>Meyer,</em> the Supreme Court itself countered this narrative. It appealed to a centuries-old tradition and called the right of parents to direct their children’s education one of “privileges long recognized at common law as essential to the orderly pursuit of happiness by free men.”</p>
            <p>What rights did parents have, according to this tradition? In the so-called “obscure list” of decisions cited by the lawyers in <em>Pierce,</em> state supreme courts repeatedly took a both/and approach: States have the authority to set minimum standards of schooling in order to form an educated citizenry, but because parents are likely to love and therefore know the temperaments, capacities, and desires of their children, they have the fundamental right to direct the particulars of their offspring’s education. Thus, state supreme courts upheld compulsory education laws (as Bowes is eager to point out) even as they ruled that parents have the right to opt their children out from certain curricula in public schools and to educate their children at home.</p>
            <p>Finally, and perhaps characteristic of the field of gender studies, Bowes finds racism and misogyny under every stone she turns, allowing fidelity to an academic discipline to drive her conclusion. We are back at this point to the Forrest Gump theory of parental rights.</p>
            <p>Most (white southerners excluded) argued for the rights of “all Americans” to direct their children’s education. Some spoke in the language of “paternal” rights—Blackstone defends “the empire of the father,” for example—but most defended “parental” rights and some even highlighted the rights of mothers. Generally speaking, the advocates of parental educational rights did not argue that the family is “beyond” state regulation. Rather, they contended that parents ought to have broad authority to fulfill their natural duties to their children. They did not reject the government’s role as such but fought to defend the right of parents from government overreach.</p>
            <p>In a notable case about whether parents could exempt their child from a mandatory “home economics” class, the Supreme Court of Nebraska singled out the public school as one of “the main bulwarks of our nation” and promised to not “knowingly do anything to undermine it.” But after praising the public schools, the court then wrote:</p>
            <blockquote><p>“We should be careful to avoid permitting our love for this noble institution to cause us to regard it as “all in all” and destroy both the God-given and constitutional right of a parent to have some voice in the bringing up and education of his children. … [I]n this age of agitation, such as the world has never known before, we want to be careful lest we carry the doctrine of governmental paternalism too far, for, after all is said and done, the prime factor in our scheme of government is the American home.”</p></blockquote>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Joseph K. Griffith II is the William Blackstone Professor of Law &amp; Society at the Ashbrook Center and assistant professor of political science at Ashland University.</p>

