          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 8 ====== -->
          <section id="ten-lies-of-therapy" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">9 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Ten Lies of Therapy Culture and the Truths that Set Us Free</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Matthew Loftus</p>

            <blockquote><p>“The spiritualizers have had their day; nowadays, the best among them appear engaged in a desperate strategy of acceptance, in the hope that by embracing doctrinal expressions of therapeutic aims they will be embraced by the therapeutics; a false hope—the therapeutics need no doctrines, only opportunities.”</p><p>– Philip Rieff, <em>Triumph of the Therapeutic</em> (1968)</p></blockquote>
            <p>Everyone can sense that something has changed profoundly in our cultural conversations about mental health, but how do we delineate these changes? The simplest definition of therapy culture revolves around medicalization, institutions, and agency—when people medicalize normal life experiences and then judge institutions on their potential for mental health impact, individuals’ sense of agency is dissolved. As one of my African colleagues observed, when “mental health” is treated as a sacred and untouchable domain of a person’s life, that person’s ability to grow and change is stifled, as the people around the individual are forced to conform to whatever expectations that person has set for himself.</p>
            <p>My book <em>Resisting Therapy Culture</em> (InterVarsity Press, 2026) is focused less on detailing what therapy culture is and more about cultivating a theologically grounded and scientifically rigorous approach to mental health. Some specific chapters address common forms of misinformation that find their way onto social media. Yet it’s helpful to have the basic contours of therapy culture set out in one place, so here are the top ten ways that therapy culture deceives people.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #1: Feeling something? It’s probably evidence of a mental health disorder.</h3>
            <p>Therapy culture begins with <em>medicalization—</em>reinterpreting something you experience as a <em>symptom</em> of a disorder. If grief is evidence of depression, anything sufficiently bad is a traumatic event. If everyday worry is a symptom of an anxiety disorder, then it’s a slippery slope to understanding more and more aspects of our lives as manifestations of illness. If someone is experiencing profound disruptions in their life as a result of a mental health disorder, this kind of awareness can be helpful. For everyone else, though, thinking “I’m sick” actually makes people sicker.</p>
            <p>For example, studies show that mental health screening programs in schools actually tend to worsen mental health outcomes—it turns out that if you ask teenagers over and over whether their sadness might be a symptom of a diagnosis, they’ll start interpreting more and more of their feelings as symptoms and make themselves sicker. Mental health experts like Lucy Foulkes, Joel Paris, and Allen Frances have been sounding the alarm about this for years, but unfortunately the medicalization is only intensifying.</p>
            <p>The truth is that we all experience ups and downs as a part of our lives. Thinking about whether or not you’re mentally healthy enough tends to make you feel worse, the more that you ruminate on it. Don’t be quick to diagnose yourself or others—and push back against screening programs that do more harm than good.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #2: Using the language of therapy to describe something makes it more real, more urgent, and places more demands on others.</h3>
            <p>Terms like “narcissist,” “gaslight,” “abuse,” and especially “trauma” get thrown around way more often than they should be. People reach for these words because they want to add a scientific imprimatur to their feelings or make their side in a personal conflict appear more righteous. This linguistic inflation is dangerous because it obscures the clinical context in which many of these words were based originally. Using these terms deceives the user into thinking that the other person in a conflict is worse than they really are and that they are helpless to do anything but escape. Other clinical-sounding terms like “Religious Trauma Syndrome,” “Highly Sensitive Person,” and “Emotionally Immature Parent” don’t even have agreed-upon definitions—they’re just concepts that people use when they want to sound more authoritative.</p>
            <p>The truth is that there are specific definitions for these terms and we decrease our discernment with indiscriminate words. Narcissistic Personality Disorder is a clinical diagnosis that only applies to a tiny fraction of the population, rather than everyone in your life who’s selfish. Gaslighting is deliberately attempting to manipulate someone’s experiences, as opposed to simply lying or stating their own opinion. If we use words like “abuse” or “trauma” to describe anything bad, at some point we have to admit that practically everyone has been traumatized or abused—at which point the words no longer mean anything specific.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #3: If something you do is related to a mental health disorder, you can’t do anything to change it, especially if it’s a trauma response.</h3>
            <p>One of the biggest temptations that people living with mental illnesses face is the temptation to surrender their agency and say, “I can’t do that because of [my diagnosis].” Or: “My illness made me do it.” There are a handful of disorders where this is true—people with schizophrenia or bipolar disorder, for example, can sometimes lose all touch with reality and do things they later regret once they’re no longer psychotic. We also recognize that illnesses like Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) can have profound physiological manifestations that are extremely difficult to control.</p>
            <p>However, someone who uses their diagnosis as an excuse—especially if that diagnosis was self-assigned or handed out in a less-than-rigorous fashion—is going to trap themselves into a smaller and smaller world as they make more and more excuses. We all struggle to do what’s right, and that struggle is harder for people who have mental illnesses. The vast majority of psychiatric illnesses involve some degree of decreased agency, which is a really difficult and painful reality. However, all of us have opportunities to strengthen our agency in one way or another and choose well for ourselves, no matter how hard that might be. (Alan Noble’s book, <em>On Getting Out of Bed,</em> is an extremely helpful meditation on this problem.)</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #4: Your primary goal in life is becoming your authentic self; anyone or anything that stands in the way of that goal is toxic.</h3>
            <p>For decades, Western culture has celebrated the idea that each person has an authentic self inside of them that must be expressed and that the chief end of every person is to discern the contours of this authentic self. Books, movies, music, and more all tell us that we should put away any constraints that hinder us from self-fulfillment and push away people that want to burden us with obligations.</p>
            <p>The truth is that we are not our own (to refer to another of Alan Noble’s books) and that we have been created by God to love Him and others. The unchosen obligations that family and friends place on us <em>(The Dignity of Dependence,</em> as Leah Libresco Sargeant calls it in her book) are good and we are all dependent on others. God has indeed created us with particular passions and personalities that we ought to pursue when we can, but the boundaries and rules that feel like hindrances are often just as good for us because they give us a stronger sense of purpose than we could ever make up for ourselves.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #5: If someone else makes you uncomfortable, the only right thing to do is to put up boundaries or demand accommodations.</h3>
            <p>When someone’s own agency is drained (see Lie #3), they demand more and more that others must change. When there is conflict or even sin in a relationship, the idea of changing one’s self or patiently bearing with another person starts to feel wrong, especially when those possibilities feel like denying one’s authentic self. When institutional rules and policies (especially in schools) start to feel difficult, the onus is now on the institution to adapt.</p>
            <p>Ivy League universities have seen a dramatic rise in requests for disability accommodations for mental health disorders in recent years. Family estrangement is on the rise, fueled in no small part by a “consumer self” who views relationships as disposable. Boundaries imposed on others and limitations imposed by individuals on themselves are becoming firmer and more extensive, choking out the possibilities for growth and joy.</p>
            <p>There are certain situations where accommodations genuinely help people to participate more in a community. Also, certain people need to set boundaries so that they will not be overrun by someone else’s sin. These situations, however, are rare and the power to invoke them shouldn’t be used flippantly. In most cases, we are called into perseverance and patience with the messy realities of life.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #6: Everyone should go to therapy.</h3>
            <p>This one is simple: No, they shouldn't. Therapy, like any other clinical intervention, has both risks and benefits. Like immunosuppressive drugs for autoimmune diseases such as lupus, therapy (and psychiatric medication) can be lifesaving for people who need it. For everyone else, though, it could be benign, but it might not be. You only need to talk to a professional if your sadness, anxiety, or other symptoms are preventing you from doing what you need to do in your life</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #7: Anything bad that happens to you can be considered trauma.</h3>
            <p>Medicalization has had a profound effect on the way our culture looks at trauma. We are now tempted to call anything bad that happens to us “trauma,” with some even arguing for the concept of “microtrauma” to describe everyday stressors. Because people with PTSD can experience profound disruptions in their minds and bodies because of things they’ve suffered, it’s easier than ever before to think of ourselves as traumatized and trace any negative feelings or behaviors back to what we suffered. If someone focuses on the bad things they’ve experienced, they can become stuck in a prison of their own making.</p>
            <p>PTSD is a real disorder of memory, but the good news is that most people who experience major, life-threatening trauma don’t develop PTSD afterwards. There are a lot of good treatments for people suffering with PTSD, most of which involve helping that person remember and understand their trauma in a way that lets them move forward with their life. The gurus and influencers who want people to magnify their trauma and use it as an excuse are telling a destructive story.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #8: Psychiatric disorders are status symbols and consumer products.</h3>
            <p>One of the more bizarre manifestations of therapy culture is the rise of people wanting (perhaps subconsciously, perhaps not) to be diagnosed with a psychiatric disorder because it grants some kind of status. Social media and peer influence are some of the biggest drivers of this phenomenon, as the terminal condition of authentic selfhood leads people to pretend to have a disorder so that they can feel unique and significant. The truth is that people with genuine mental health disorders are suffering immensely. They can be crowded out of the mental health system (and public discourse) when people who are otherwise functional medicalize aspects of their personalities.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #9: Your parents (and maybe your pastor) did this to you.</h3>
            <p>This is one of the oldest lies of therapy culture. Ever since Sigmund Freud gave us the genuine insight that things like childhood sexual abuse could have profound effects on someone’s mental health as an adult, people have constantly sought to blame their parents or their church for their mental health problems as an adult. It feels so easy in retrospect to assign responsibility, especially when influencers tout “gentle parenting” that gives people a taste of what they wish they’d had as kids.</p>
            <p>This has been such a problem for psychology that decades ago, researchers began to study what kinds of things do have lifelong effects. Kids who experience abuse, neglect (including not setting appropriate boundaries), and profound emotional manipulation are at higher risk for mental illnesses as adults. However, “good enough” parents who avoid these behaviors and provide authoritative guidance while expressing love and care to their kids can be reassured that it’s not their fault.</p>
            <p>How and why mental illnesses develop in certain people but not others is a tricky question. It’s clear that there are some genetic and biological risk factors that are often activated by traumatic experiences or other major life stressors. For some people, there’s no way to avoid developing certain diseases while others are the victims of inexcusable sin that magnifies whatever underlying predisposition they were born with. We have to be careful about the stories we tell ourselves and ensure that they are helping us move forward rather than immiserating ourselves by ruminating about the past.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Lie #10: Church should be more like therapy.</h3>
            <p>When therapy is no longer a practice and instead becomes a culture, our value systems tend to judge any institution by whether it is appropriately therapeutic. Since the Bible does teach about God’s healing power, it is extremely tempting to look at all of Scripture and Christian doctrine through a therapeutic lens.</p>
            <p>In practice, this often means excising or neutering doctrines that make people uncomfortable (see Lie #5). Sin is no longer a violation of God’s just law but merely turning away from His love. Church music focuses less on the nature of God and more about how He has made us feel good. Pastors feel pressured to be more like therapists.</p>
            <p>The Church of Jesus Christ doesn’t exist to solve people’s problems; it exists to worship and proclaim God’s love for us through preaching, singing, and the Eucharist. Many people have their personal, even mental health problems, solved (at least in part) when they go to church because a healthy church that is sticking to its mandate is naturally going to be a healing community. However, expecting churches to do for people what therapy does for them is both wrong and unfair. Therapists should be therapists and do their limited job well. Churches should be churches and fulfill the unique calling that God has placed on them.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Conclusion: Resistance is Not Futile</h3>
            <p>Therapy culture makes people psychologically sicker by draining away their agency. It deceives people into pathologizing their normal, everyday lives. The Church’s job is not to try to beat therapists at their own game—Rieff observed 60 years ago that such a “solution” was anything but. Rather, the Church must proclaim the whole truth of Scripture as it does the things that no other institution can do, proclaiming the power of the risen Christ to heal us from our sin by faith, through grace.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Matthew Loftus grew up in a family of 15 children and completed his medical training in Baltimore, Maryland. Since 2015, he and his family have lived in East Africa, where he currently teaches and practices family medicine at a mission hospital. His work has appeared in outlets such as <em>The New York Times</em>, <em>The Atlantic</em>, <em>The New Atlantis</em>, and <em>Mere Orthodoxy</em>. <em>Resisting Therapy Culture</em> (InterVarsity Press, 2026) is his first book.</p>

