    <h2 class="section-title">Fogbound</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Mischa Willett</p>

            <p><em>after Richard Shelton</em></p>
            <p>After each shuttering<br>when the sign goes up at Anacortes<br>blinking closed alert we’re closed<br>do not come here a few<br>of us imagine we’re marooned.</p>
            <p>The gravel pulls back in the shushing<br>tidal rumble, tosses back<br>beneath the dock scatterings<br>of shells at our feet.</p>
            <p>We almost follow them home.<br>If not for the rocks<br>we might stay and sleep beneath<br>the mist, rocked by kelp<br>lullabied by crying gulls<br>who sound alarm fish alarm dark<br>alarm crow</p>
            <p>But we are not so drunk<br>as that nor romantic.<br>Beds await us each and sleep<br>and families who will wonder why we<br>fishermen none smell so like silver longing<br>the ache and sweet of salt, the sea.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Mischa Willett is director of the MFA in creative writing at Whitworth University. He has written poems, translations, and essays for <em>Books and Culture</em>, <em>Chronicle of Higher Education</em>, <em>First Things</em>, <em>Comment Magazine</em>, <em>Front Porch Republic</em>, and others. This poem from his latest poetry collection, <em>This Gift Card Has Already Been Redeemed</em> (Paraclete Press, 2026), is reproduced with permission from the publisher.</p>

