          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 10 ====== -->
          <section id="wired-for-postliterate-wonder" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">11 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Wired for Post-Literate Wonder</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Samuel D. James</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Aaron Cline Hanbury. <em>Wired for Wonder: Dispatches on Technology, Culture, God, and Self</em>. Broadleaf Books, 2026. $27.99. 179 pp.</p>
            <p>Promoters of Big Tech do not see great value in singular, sustained trains of thought. We know this primarily because the products they invent prevent it. Almost every piece of internet-connected technology released since the iPhone has made it incrementally harder for people to invest their attention in a single idea or argument. Instead, as Nicholas Carr demonstrated in his landmark 2010 book <em>The Shallows,</em> digital media has “computerized” our attention spans. Today, modern people are better than ever at pivoting attention from one thing to the next very quickly and scanning a page or story for the desired particulars. The large language model era promises to supercharge these abilities.</p>
            <p>But these abilities are the native skillset of a computer, not the human mind. Carr’s conclusion was that the brain immersed in digital media would begin to operate in a way very different than the brains of human beings in generations past. Even our recent ancestors were limited by both space and time in what they paid attention to. The smartphone-era brain, by contrast, would be one retrained by technology into becoming a “juggler”—very good at throwing many things in the air and holding a few for a brief moment, but very bad at devoting real time and energy toward a single object.</p>
            <p>Carr was right. Since the mass adoption of smartphones, social media, and the online attention economy, the emerging generation’s capacity for profound attention and contemplation has cratered. In her July 2026 essay for <em>The Atlantic,</em> “The End of Reading Is Here,” Rose Horowitch describes American society as “post-literate” rather than “illiterate.” It’s not that Americans don’t read, she argues. It’s that the definition of “reading” has changed. She writes: “People are bombarded with emails, text messages, X posts, Reddit threads, Instagram captions. This explosion of textual fragments has come at the expense of devoting sustained attention to longer written works that convey rich and complicated information.”</p>
            <p>Horowitch’s distinction between illiterate and “post-literate” is important because it corrects a misconception about what the digital age really does. It is not that tech natives are unwilling or unable to read a book, watch a movie, listen to a lecture, etc. Rather, it’s that tech natives feel a compulsion to experience these things in particular ways: piecemeal, with small chunks at a time, and interrupting or pausing them to check notifications or refresh the feed frequently. It’s not that the film industry is on the brink of extinction; it’s that the movies themselves are tinted, shot, and even written in a way that accommodates the distracted viewer. There’s still a robust market for TED Talks or heady podcast conversations, but those things are more likely to be shorter, sped up, and put on video than they were a decade ago.</p>
            <p>But what about books? Can books be “packaged” in a way to accommodate the anxious generation? And if so, what do both author and reader stand to gain and lose by such an accommodation?</p>
            <p>I mention all this because Aaron Cline Hanbury’s book <em>Wired for Wonder: Dispatches on Technology, Culture, God, and the Self</em> is a tech-critical work that is wrapped up in a tech-friendly format. Hanbury, founding editor of <em>Common Good Magazine,</em> is no fan of Big Tech. His slim volume summons the spirits of Carr, Albert Borgmann, and Wendell Berry to indict the digital age for its inhumanity. There are great lines and powerful illustrations throughout the book’s twenty-seven essays. But there’s also a structure and a flavor to them that feels less like a reclamation project for the great intellectual achievement that is the book, and more like an admission that the future of writing, even tech-critical writing, is bloggy.</p>
            <p>Hanbury has a talent for turning apparently mundane human experiences into objects of insightful thinking. One of my favorite chapters in the book is a memoir-like section where Hanbury talks about staring at the back of his father’s ears during road trips (since there was no iPad to occupy him). Those hours of unspoken for attention became knowledge and memories of his dad that maybe no one else in the world has.</p>
            <p>In another chapter, Hanbury critiques typical evangelical mantras on technology. Gospel expositions and hopeful attempts to use tech well have their place, he says, but “the fragmentation that our technological movement pushes on us can only be solved by a putting right of the world.” He’s right. Christians cannot get away from the intellectual, relational, and spiritual implications of technology by hiding behind platitudes. Some things just need to be resisted.</p>
            <p>And yet, ever since Marshall McLuhan reminded the world that the medium is the message, the question has always lingered: How does one really resist? The complications multiply because even the ways we critique technology tend to be influenced by our technological world. This is true for all, but in the case of <em>Wired for Wonder,</em> it’s especially pressing. I’m concerned that in the course of making several salient arguments against the bending of the world toward computer values, <em>Wired for Wonder</em> turns away from the best literary form, and ends up reading much like a collection of web articles.</p>
            <p>Example #1: The book’s first entry, titled “Simulation Game.” Here Hanbury offers a critique of AI that many, including myself, will cheer. At one point, he writes: “Technology represents something we <em>experience,</em> and even then, a more tangible description would be something closer to <em>view.”</em> This distinction between viewing and experiencing is very interesting, particularly with regard to AI. Unfortunately, the essay closes just a few sentences later, with this idea almost entirely unexplored. The whole experience feels very familiar to someone who has read their weight in web articles, which often cut off right when they’re getting interesting. To get such an experience in a print book is more than a little disorienting.</p>
            <p>Example #2: On a related note, several of the book’s entries are extremely short. I counted at least 3 or 4 chapters that are less than 1,000 words long. This is not a problem per se, but again, almost all of these abbreviated chapters leave interesting ideas underdeveloped for unclear reasons. The uneven length, combined with a lack of cross-referencing between the chapters, allows you to pick up the book at almost any given chapter and dive right in easily—a very appealing attribute if you’re unable or unwilling to devote time up-front.</p>
            <p>Example #3: There are several chapters that feel out of place, as if they were composed separately from the rest of the manuscript and then inserted later. An essay on the MeToo movement feels awkward and asymmetrical with the overall genre of the book (curiously, the essay does not discuss the role that social media technology played in the nature and morality of the movement). Once again, this asymmetry is greatly relieved if one engages with this book the way they more or less would with a website or blog, picking their way topically through the content. But it is hard to miss on a dedicated read-through.</p>
            <p>None of these examples mean that <em>Wired for Wonder</em> is a bad book. Rather, taken together, these features raise the question of what <em>kind</em> of book it is. My guess? It’s the kind of book that will become far more common in the years to come: Brief, episodic, and wider than deep. These books have existed for a long time, of course. And it’s always been true that the economic tides of book-buying trends wash in and out regularly. But I suspect that this particular style is well attuned to the changes that are happening to readers more generally. Shorter chapters, for example, seem like an eminently reasonable and effective way to encourage reader’s perseverance while also acknowledging the disruptions they are bringing with them each sitting. Structuring chapters more independently of each other, rather than referencing concepts introduced earlier, or building on previous examples or quotes, seems likewise to be a common-sense way of conceding that skimming readers are more likely to keep going if they don’t feel lost.</p>
            <p>Even as the publisher side of me understands the economic reality at play here, the writer and reader side of me is starting to sweat. I do wonder what will be lost if authors and publishers eschew complex topics, deep dives, careful argumentation, and incremental logic. While these things may indeed be much more challenging in a distracted technological era, I also think they bring more value to it as well.</p>
            <p>There’s something to be said for the paragraph, for example; for building a series of sentences together into a coherent line of thought, then stringing these bundles together until you have a single, unified idea that is both the sum and more than the sum of its parts. There’s something to be said for the chapter, for the careful building out of an idea into different applications and meanings, separated by typesetting in a way that teaches the reader not just what to think but how to think. Paragraphs, chapters, subheadings, footnotes, opening illustrations—none are magical, but they are all part of a literary inheritance that, for better and worse, helped build our world and the way we think about it.</p>
            <p>My favorite thing about <em>Wired for Wonder</em> is the title. Yes, and amen. We are indeed wired for wonder. And that’s why I think we should not be too intimidated by the work of trying to communicate big, wondrous ideas to skimming, distracted readers. It may not feel natural anymore. But it’s OK. They’re made for it.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Samuel D. James is associate acquisitions editor for Crossway Books and the author of <em>Digital Liturgies: Rediscovering Christian Wisdom in an Online Age</em>.</p>

