            <p class="rd-essay-n">12 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Abolitionist Theology Wasn’t Very Evangelical—But It Was Apocalyptic</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Daniel K. Williams</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Kevin M. Burton. <em>Apocalyptic Abolitionism: How Millennialists Helped Abolish Slavery and Reform America</em>. New York University Press, 2026. $30.00. 336 pp.</p>
            <p>Abolitionism was deeply countercultural in antebellum America, even in the states that had already abolished slavery. According to Kevin Burton’s <em>Apocalyptic Abolitionism,</em> only one or two percent of white Americans living in the North on the eve of the Civil War were abolitionists.</p>
            <p>That doesn’t mean that most northern Christians (including northern Episcopalians, Presbyterians, and Baptists) were pro-slavery. Most were not. But the vast majority of those who objected to slavery did not call for its immediate end or make it a test of Christian fellowship. Instead, they settled for gradual emancipation, often coupled with calls to send newly freed African Americans to Africa, where emancipated Blacks would not challenge white prejudices with a demand for equal American citizenship rights. Gradualists’ form of quiet antislavery sentiment was perfectly compatible with mainstream party politics and was unlikely to make its practitioners social pariahs, at least if they lived in the North.</p>
            <p>Abolitionists were more radical. They demanded an immediate end to slavery. And unlike gradual emancipationists who supported colonization schemes in the hope that emancipated slaves could be sent to Africa, many abolitionists believed in the possibility of a fully egalitarian, interracial society. In keeping with their commitment to human equality, several also supported women’s right to vote and preach to mixed audiences of men and women—radical concepts at the time.</p>
            <p>Abolitionists didn’t mind championing deeply unpopular ideas. They didn’t care that immediate abolition would upend the American economy by instantly wiping out the nation’s most valuable economic commodity—that is, the enslaved humans who were collectively valued at four billion, which was nearly equal to the nation’s entire annual GDP, by some measures. They didn’t care that it would likely destroy the textile trade that the northern industrial economy depended on. They didn’t care that it would probably lead to a demand from four million newly freed African Americans to receive voting rights and other legal protections that few whites of the time were willing to accept.</p>
            <p>All that mattered, they believed, was that slavery was evil and therefore had to cease. Any compromise with evil was anathema.</p>
            <p>The vast majority of white American Christians never adopted such radical views. But that begs the question: What beliefs might have led a select few to break with the majority and join the abolitionist cause? Was there any particular Christian theology that led a few Christians to become radical abolitionist activists, even when the vast majority of the nation’s white Christians did not?</p>
            <p>The answer is far from obvious. The abolitionists were pretty diverse denominationally. A disproportionately high number were Quakers—which is not too surprising, considering that the idea of human equality was a foundational belief for the sect. Indeed, the Society of Friends was the first American denomination to pass resolutions against slavery. But beyond that, abolitionists seem to have come from a wide variety of denominational traditions.</p>
            <p>Conversions to Unitarianism were common enough among abolitionists. For instance, both William Lloyd Garrison and Lydia Maria Child became Unitarians as adults, after spending their earlier life in much more orthodox evangelical backgrounds. As a result, some historians have seen a common denominator there. Perhaps liberal theological traditions (like Unitarianism) that grounded moral claims in human reason or intuition rather than in biblical authority were more conducive to abolitionism. Molly Oshatz argued in <em>Slavery and Sin</em> (2011) that American Christian antislavery activism depended on a liberal “Golden Rule” theology that was at odds with a nineteenth-century evangelical Protestant hermeneutic that could not square an absolutist stand against slavery with biblical commands for slaves to obey their masters, such as the one found in Colossians 4:1. Only a liberal biblical hermeneutic that used Jesus’s Golden Rule to reinterpret or override specific biblical teaching on slavery could make this leap.</p>
            <p>But the abolitionist movement also included plenty of more theologically orthodox evangelicals—and even some “fundamentalist”-like biblical literalists, to use a twentieth-century term. John Brown, an apocalyptic Calvinist, was one. So was Elijah Lovejoy, an ordained Presbyterian minister who was killed by a mob in Illinois for editing an abolitionist newspaper.</p>
            <p>Traditionally, historians attributed the antislavery fervor of evangelical Protestants such as Charles Finney and Harriet Beecher Stowe to the revivalism of the Second Great Awakening. Some of the Christians who embraced a new Wesleyan theology of perfectionism, an egalitarian vision of human equality before God, or a democratic interest in social reform joined the anti-slavery movement, and some of those opponents of slavery became full-blown abolitionists.</p>
            <p>This was the view I learned in graduate school from works such as Robert Abzug’s <em>Cosmos Crumbling</em> (1994), and it was the view that I taught when I first began covering this topic in my U.S. history classes. We cannot understand nineteenth-century white Christian concerns about slavery apart from the era’s other new evangelical reform causes (such as the temperance movement), because all stemmed from the same optimistic desire to protect the home, honor human dignity, and remake human society along Christian perfectionist lines.</p>
            <p>But Burton argues that the vast majority of northern evangelical reformers had too much faith in existing American institutions to adopt a view as radical as immediate abolition of slavery. In the 1840s, neither of the nation’s two major political parties were abolitionist, nor were the nation’s largest denominations. Evangelicals were too committed to their churches and their missionary societies to adopt a countercultural stance that would have alienated them from most of their fellow American believers. Evangelicalism—at least, evangelicalism in its most common nineteenth-century American guise—was not the chief source of abolitionism, Burton argues.</p>
            <p>But neither was theological liberalism, as Oshatz posited. Based on a detailed study of the theological beliefs and denominational backgrounds of leaders in the Liberty Party (an antislavery party of the 1840s that served as a third-party alternative to the Whigs and Democrats), Burton concludes that many abolitionists were part of a theological movement that was deeply literalistic in its interpretation of the biblical text. But they were not mainstream evangelicals. Instead, they were millenarian apocalyptists who expected Jesus’s immediate return—likely in 1844, as the Adventist William Miller predicted.</p>
            <p>Burton, an assistant professor of church history at Seventh-day Adventist Theological Seminary at Andrews University, acknowledges that previous scholarship on abolitionism has almost completely ignored the Adventists. That’s not because previous historians have never written about abolitionists who were Adventists, he says. It’s instead because when historians have written about prominent Adventist abolitionists—such as Gerrit Smith, the Liberty Party’s nominee for president in 1848 and a congressional representative from Massachusetts elected on the Liberty Party ticket—they have not thought their subjects’ Adventist faith was significant enough to mention, even though it was quite likely the source of their abolitionist beliefs.</p>
            <p>Historians have had a blind spot when it comes to the Adventists, primarily because they have accepted the stereotype that the followers of William Miller who expected the immediate return of Jesus were other-worldly apocalyptists who had no interest in social reform, Burton thinks. They have imagined that only the Protestant postmillennialists who focused on building God’s kingdom on earth cared enough about reforming society to embrace the antislavery cause. But these stereotypes are wrong, Burton argues. Adventists were unanimously opposed to slavery, and many of them believed they had a duty to do what they could to end it.</p>
            <p>Historians might think that Adventists who expected Jesus to return to earth within a few months would not run for Congress or organize a national political party, but some did exactly that. They played a leading role in organizing the Liberty Party, which earned enough votes in New York in the 1844 election to deprive Whig candidate Henry Clay of the presidency and throw the election to James Knox Polk. The reason they did so, Burton says, is precisely because of their apocalypticism. If Jesus were coming back immediately, there was no time to wait for a strategy of gradual reform. They had to end slavery immediately to get the nation ready for the Second Coming.</p>
            <p>Burton supports his argument with impressive archival evidence and an appendix listing several hundred “known Adventist abolitionists of prominence.” Before reading this book, I had not known that Anthony Burns (whom I routinely cover in my US history courses) was an Adventist. Nor had I known that the Liberty Party selected an Adventist as its presidential nominee.</p>
            <p>I am not persuaded that Burton’s argument explains all of American abolitionism, but I do think that he has demonstrated the role that expectations of imminent divine judgment played in encouraging people to act against slavery. I have long been struck by how prominent apocalyptic theologies were in the thinking of some of the instigators of nineteenth-century slave revolts, such as Denmark Vesey and Nat Turner. The same is true of Sojourner Truth. Burton has demonstrated that a similar apocalypticism motivated many of the white abolitionists of the time.</p>
            <p>I also think that he provides a convincing (and charitable) explanation of why most non-apocalyptic evangelical Christians of the 1840s and 1850s did not become abolitionists. They wanted to end slavery, but they were too committed to existing institutions to embrace a countercultural movement that would have disrupted those institutions, Burton argues. But Adventists did not have those institutional commitments. If Jesus was coming back immediately, there was no time to wait for the Whig Party to slowly adopt an antislavery platform or for the Presbyterian Church to come to a consensus against slavery. If a denomination did not join the abolitionist cause, Christians should leave that denomination, the Adventists thought—and if a political party refused to become abolitionist, they should likewise renounce the party. Those institutions would never survive God’s imminent judgment anyway—so if Jesus’s return was near, the only thing that mattered was getting right with Jesus by ridding the nation of slavery.</p>
            <p>Burton’s argument explains one strand of abolitionism, but it doesn’t quite explain the abolitionism of non-apocalyptic groups such as the Quakers, the Unitarians, or the Transcendentalists. To more fully explain abolitionist theology in all of its many strands, I think that we can expand on Burton’s argument by saying that apocalypticism was only one part of a much larger phenomenon: the willingness to stand against American cultural institutions in both the political and religious realm. Nineteenth-century Quakers were not apocalyptists, but they did see themselves as a set-apart people who wore distinctive clothing, adopted a distinctive pattern of speech, and, above all, held to a distinctive, uncompromising set of ethics. Likewise, William Lloyd Garrison was not an apocalyptist, but his willingness to burn the American Constitution in a public protest against slavery clearly marked him as a dissenter from American institutions.</p>
            <p>As Burton reminds us, most evangelical Protestants never became abolitionists, because they were instinctively conservative. They wanted to preserve, not dismantle, American institutions. They weren’t going to burn the American Constitution, separate from mainstream evangelical Protestant denominations, or reject both of the nation’s major political parties. As he suggests in his introduction, contemporary American evangelicals are heirs to this conservative, pro-institutional legacy. Our theological forebears were not the abolitionists.</p>
            <p>That raises an uncomfortable question: Should we be more radical? Do we need to change our theology to bring it more in line with that of the nineteenth-century abolitionists?</p>
            <p>The answer, I think, is both yes and no. Burton’s book reminds us of the radical cost of following a Christian ethic when it’s unpopular, and it suggests that American evangelicals (like other Christians) face the constant temptation to become too comfortable with American institutions in both the ecclesiastical and political realm. It’s hard to take a firm moral stance on an issue when neither of the nation’s major political parties is willing to do so. It may be even harder to do so when one’s own denomination doesn’t show much interest in the cause either. A Christian who wants to follow Jesus may have to endure social ostracism not only from society at large but even from within the church. When it comes to our own thinking about moral issues, we should be willing to draw on biblical precepts to be deeply countercultural and reject the assumptions of all of the major political parties as well as much of American Christianity. If we do not do so, we will unthinkingly imbibe the prejudices and blind spots of our age, just as many nineteenth-century Christians did.</p>
            <p>But while it’s one thing to insist on clear-eyed moral and theological thinking, it’s another to hold to an uncompromising apocalypticism when voting for a candidate or selecting a church. All churches are filled with sinners, and no denomination has a perfect record when it comes to applications of its theological views on cultural issues. I think that we can’t simply decide that the nineteenth-century evangelical institution-builders who chose to preserve denominational unity rather than split their churches by embracing a radical abolitionist stance were cowardly or worldly appeasers. The New Testament has a great deal to say about preserving the unity of the faith and showing forbearance to one another in love. And from a historical perspective, we can say that since Jesus did not return to earth in 1844, political and religious institution-building ultimately accomplished more than the Adventists expected. After all, the president who eventually issued the Emancipation Proclamation was not the radical Liberty Party candidate Gerrit Smith; it was instead the antislavery Whig Abraham Lincoln, who chose to work within the existing two-party political system in the 1840s instead of joining the Adventist abolitionists.</p>
            <p>Burton reminds us why most American Christians did not become abolitionists in the 1840s—and why a group of apocalyptic Adventists did. His historical narrative should be a warning for all Christians about the moral blind spots that may come from becoming too invested in the institutions and cultural assumptions of our own moment. But if there are dangers in becoming too comfortable with the prevailing culture, there are other pitfalls that come from a politics of radical apocalypticism. A wise Christian will need to be wary of both.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Daniel K. Williams teaches American history at Ashland University and is the author of multiple books, including <em>The Politics of the Cross: A Christian Alternative to Partisanship</em> and <em>The Search for a Rational Faith: Reason and Belief in the History of American Christianity</em>.</p>

