          <section id="liturgies-of-the-fireflies" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">13 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Liturgies of the Fireflies</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Emily Carrington</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Martin Shaw. <em>Liturgies of the Wild: Myths That Make Us</em>. Sentinel, 2026. $30.00. 256 pp.</p>
            <p>The sun just set over my corner of the Midwest. The newly arrived darkness offers a calm and quiet contrast to those pre-dawn anxious moments that have riddled me lately. As I sit on my back deck, I watch the sky twinkle with thousands of fireflies stretching over my property, up in the tree line, and over the Amish field next to my house. The air is warm but comfortable, and the real sounds of frogs, crickets, and owls accompany my instrumental Spotify playlist. Suddenly I am dizzyingly aware of the present while time and place start to collapse.</p>
            <p>Tonight I am staring at my backyard, while my husband watches the World Cup just a few feet away inside, and my daughters sleep upstairs. I can smell, hear, and feel the present. But simultaneously, in my mind I am in the passenger seat of my dad’s red Durango, across town and looking out on a different field—no husband, no children, no house. I was leaving this town, this county, and this state, and the whole world of myths that made me. Vivid memories flood in, beckoned by the fireflies.</p>
            <p>It was another warm evening in late June, much like this one. My dad had stopped for a piece of pie and a coffee at the rest-stop Perkins where I waited tables that summer. He was driving me back to where I was staying that night, when we suddenly gasped. The corn field to our right was glittering with fireflies like we had never seen before. He stopped the car and we sat there a few quiet moments in wonder before he drove on.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>I experienced an incredibly rooted childhood. Moving for the first time meant packing some belongings and living in a college dorm room not far from home while my childhood bedroom remained untouched. I had never really “moved.” But that June when my dad and I saw the fireflies, I was weeks away from getting married, moving to Texas, and starting a new life. Those last days in a familiar setting felt fleeting and stuck all at the same time. Anxiety filled my body, and I had little room to marvel at the amazing journey before me—the delight of a wedding, the excitement of college graduation, a new future all my own. I was filled with fear and walked with stoic steps toward the unknown.</p>
            <p>It was my dad who taught me to stop and wonder, to make room for awe. He never overdid it; he simply marveled himself. He was never one to overexplain and overanalyze. He remains to this day someone who is willing to simply delight. As he stopped the car that night, it wasn’t profound or didactic. It was just the right thing to do. And in God’s providence I was present and marveled at the fireflies he had raised me to notice.</p>
            <p>Fifteen years, two children, and six moves after that night, I ended up right back home in Ohio. As I write this, only a few miles and seventeen years separate me from that glorious field. I didn’t even realize the fireflies were marking my goodbye back then until they welcomed me home tonight.</p>
            <p>The past seventeen years have been a journey that has left me with what Martin Shaw would call, in his <em>Liturgies of the Wild,</em> a “limp” and a “gift.” Part of this gift is that in the darkness there is beauty. I pray that I too will raise my children to see the fireflies in the darkness. I don’t have to usher my daughters to this awareness alone, as their grandfather now quietly teaches them to gently catch fireflies, enjoy their beauty, and set them free again. I pray for them that our field of fireflies grows into mythic proportions in their minds, and that when the day comes for the fireflies to wave them goodbye, their light will later call them home. May this be the call that connects my children to their land and their people, and may they know the delight of their grandfather, the man who looks at their dirty little faces, much like he did at mine and my sister’s, bursting with awe.</p>
            <p>“We are being prepared for eternity every time we behold beauty,” reflects Shaw.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>Reading Shaw felt at times like sitting with my own grandfather, comforted by the gentle yet harried wisdom of the old. Though Shaw is nothing like my late grandfather—a Methodist blue-collar roofer from Northeast Ohio—reading <em>Liturgies of the Wild</em> felt much like a conversation with him: an offering of stories, meanderings, and disconnected pieces. The type of conversation where you at first walk away not entirely sure what you have learned but knowing that you were made better for it.</p>
            <p>The lack of clarity is not due to a lack of depth; perhaps it is because of it. Shaw does not present his liturgies in a tidy box with a ten-step plan or system we can simply follow to enlightenment. Instead, Shaw is inviting us out of our typical order to examine the substance of stories of which we are made.</p>
            <p>Here Shaw avoids two common errors that plague most modern attempts at self-understanding. The first is that many undertake an overly inward examination to achieve personal authenticity. The second is a seemingly opposite approach that sends us outward to find meaning and understanding anywhere and everywhere else—it is that now clichéd journey to disconnect, ascend, or transform the self outside of one’s own context and reality. But I would argue these are not opposite journeys—they are rooted in the same modern axiom: You can be anything you want to be.</p>
            <p>Shaw, though, wants us to examine <em>how</em> we are created before we can be creative. In submission to the Creator, he turns our attention to the earth, the body, the community, and the myths that make us: “Far from being untruths, [myths].... show us what to defend, what to care about, the need for pluck, sacrifice, and generosity. In the writ largeness of their narratives we can locate our own stories, like a river finding the sea.” Untethered from our own story and the pieces that make us, we will never know ourselves.</p>
            <p>In his robust and welcomed defense of myth as truth, Shaw pushes past fairy tales as distant dreams and uses them to root us in our time and place. Myths are not lies, he insists. Instead, they are somehow a bridge to fuller more contextualized truths. So he moves from myth to start with man’s initial “ingredients,” which he identifies as “mud” and “holy breath.” According to Shaw, the mud man and the holy breath hold a tension. If we were all mud, we would not be animate. Yet if we were all breath, we would be an untethered passionate spirit unable to mature. It is myth that must hold the mud and breath together.</p>
            <p>As Shaw takes us on the journey of our making, he punctuates his thoughts with stories. He uses a variety of tales with complex meanings and hidden symbolism that challenge our assumed structures. But Shaw does not simply deconstruct our understanding of the world to leave us unmade—instead he teaches his reader to read better, to learn better, to see better. He teaches to behold. He does this by unpacking the symbolism, the truth, and the thread—but with the warning that nothing holds together neatly.</p>
            <p>We all carry stories, he reminds, some well-rehearsed and others tucked away. Some are forgotten completely. Some parts of our stories don’t seem important for a season until the unfolding suddenly rewrites our understanding.</p>
            <p>A few chapters in, I found myself reaching for this book in moments when the world was dark and quiet, yet my soul was unsettled; those literal moments before dawn, when you wake up too early and might reach for your phone to doomscroll to numb the anxiety. Oddly, Shaw's meanderings brought me comfort and peace in those moments, even when clear understanding felt elusive.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>It is unrestrained beauty that points us to the creator who formed us with the Holy Breath and leads us to the quintessential man Jesus, Shaw says. But it is here that I, neither a puritan nor a mystic, must part ways with Shaw, ever so carefully. While Shaw is clearly saturated by the Word in such a way that it simply overflows from his lips and infiltrates his stories, <em>Liturgies of the Wild</em> fails to fully embrace the mysterious power of the authority of the Word of God. I may sound like a Bible-thumping Protestant, but Shaw’s subtle critique of a text-based religiosity risks undervaluing the source, authority, and necessity of the text itself. He does not need to do this. His demonstration that story, myth, and language animate our beings is entirely consistent with the living Word that brings life, beauty, and majesty.</p>
            <p>Before unpacking this criticism, it is important to understand what Shaw does uphold. He does not reject the holy Scriptures. Nor does he reject the beauty and power in words themselves. He sees that “by these words somehow my imagination has met your imagination and some third thing is happening. We can be visioned both by words spoken and words read.” There is power in the Word.</p>
            <p>It is also very clear that he knows, loves, and uses the words of Scripture. This book is saturated with biblical stories, gospel truths, and the word of God itself. My careful critique here is that he is not tethered or limited or even awed by it as the source and authority of gospel truth.</p>
            <p>We see this in the chapter on evil. Shaw accuses the reformational Anglicans of abandoning the “ecclesiastical counter-magic to protect the congregation from their neighbors,” such as holy water, the sign of the cross, and the richness of the image and replacing them with a “rather stark agenda—prayer in cold pews and the thin cry for repentance.”</p>
            <p>As he continues, Shaw paints these Reformers as finding themselves playing whack-a-mole with magic and spirits of the age. The church was so “diligently pruned, when the magical thinking of the people was given such heavy penalty, then the church inadvertently opened a back door to animism.” Shaw continues in a similar vein: “God’s inscrutable visage peering down on the chilly-pewed churchgoer was less compelling than the attentive ear and the healing poultice of hedge women.” And so it goes; apparently it is Protestants who gave rise to both the cold, modern, age of reason and the pagan frenzy of witchcraft.</p>
            <p>Of course, however narrow or broad Shaw’s critique of the Reformation, even the most puritanical Reformers would tell a different story. Theirs was not an intentional attack or stripping away of the magic and wonder, but a recentering of the Word of God as a necessary means of grace through which we meet the Divine himself. If this re-centered authority of Scripture drives out superstitions, incantations, and unnecessary spiritual burdens, so be it.</p>
            <p>Shaw’s perspective and description of the Church paints the Middle Ages as a time when the veil was thinner and our worship practices more aligned with God’s presence. But this is a naively nostalgic view of history—a tempting one in our hypermodern age of reason, but still overly simplistic. The sign of the cross, the holy water, and the eucharist hold no power except through the authority and presence of Christ himself as the Word whose purpose is to attest to him. The Word is not only primary, authoritative, and central; it is also necessary as part of the encounter with the God-Man. It is the Word that became flesh and dwelt among us. Is not such presence an incredible marvel that no symbolism can replace?</p>
            <p>But Protestants should also heed Shaw’s warning that we need not eliminate all symbolism to protect the beauty of the Word. As Shaw demonstrates, humans are both praise-makers and co-creators, and we are called to respond to the Living Word in both these roles. We should not be taken up with distractions, nor should we be stripped of our participation in the beautiful worship of the Creator. Somehow, we must bathe ourselves in story, reconnect with our limited bodies, and read Scripture with a dynamic wonder, recognizing that the Word of God does not limit God. It logically cannot—it is, rather, a supernatural self-revelation of God by God. The limits come, rather, from us and our finite understanding of God’s Word.</p>
            <p>As I said before, I am neither a puritan nor a mystic.</p>
            <p>***</p>
            <p>While Shaw does not ask this question directly, his discussion of the Reformation points to another conversation unfolding right now. Many seem to be pondering whether we are living at an end point, hinge point, or something entirely new in history. Like the time of the Reformation, we are suddenly faced with new technology, new access, new religiosity, new economic structures, new everything. Uncomfortable with this brave new world, some are asking: is this the natural end to which the Word-based Reformers were always pointing? Was this always how modernity must end? Disenchanted, disconnected, and ruled by reason?</p>
            <p>Perhaps, but we must pause before we blame the “others” for the thing we all hold in contempt. Many Protestants feel as equally foreign in today’s hyper-rational tech-driven commodified world as our Catholic and Orthodox brethren. I would be so bold as to suggest that this is not the natural end of Protestantism; this present ill is entirely of itself. The problem with our modern age is not that we are too deeply rooted in the Word of God to see ourselves rightly. Nor is it that Protestant worship lacks magic; many Protestants are quite enchanted. Protestants, Catholics, and Orthodox alike cannot be at ease with a world so disconnected from itself. It is not Word-based worship that pulls us away from reality; it should instead connect us to it.</p>
            <p>Yet we are also at a crucial time of reckoning with what it means to be a human being. Shaw warns: “the more Christianity jettisons its wyrd, nervously polls for popularity, abandons its saints and delicious eccentricities, the more it opens the door to pasty-faced New Age woo-woo. Because underneath such an appeal is the longing for the kind of truths Christianity has always embodied. We should hold our nerve, and also not be naive about the time we are entering.”</p>
            <p>I join Shaw in his warnings against falling into New Age woo-woo, but I am concerned that without the Word governing, informing, illuminating, and limiting our discernment, we might mistake Satan’s flimsy imitations for the presence of God. Not all magic is good magic, and not all revelations are sent by the Godhead.</p>
            <p>Which brings me to a second pause while journeying with Shaw: his chapter on dreams. To set out to search for visions in our dreams invites the temptation to be persuaded by false revelation. This does not mean Shaw is not careful. He warns against seeking endless interpretations and reading dream dictionaries. He heeds the criticisms of the desert fathers. He draws from biblical examples and seeks submission to the Creator. He declares “You know you have a vision when it bumps into obstacles and doesn’t go up into smoke.” Maybe, as long as one of those obstacles is the Holy Word of God itself—a necessary warning Shaw does not explicitly offer.</p>
            <p>That I, a lifelong liturgical sacramental Protestant, would differ with Shaw on these accounts is not a surprise. Still, I urge other Protestants to join me and sit with Shaw and engage. Because for all of my differences with Shaw, I find myself better for journeying with him. Especially powerful is his final story in the epilogue. Through his story of Parzival, written during his own unmaking of sorts, Shaw demonstrates the journey to Christ-like compassion to which we are all called.</p>
            <p>Parzival was a young noble who did not know he was the son of a king. But contrary to what one might expect, this story is not a call for Parzival to find, embrace, and live out his noble identity. Instead, this is a story of how Parzival came to see the earth’s wounds and respond in compassion. The young boy was raised in the woods, then left his mother to become a knight of Camelot, in spite of her wishes that he avoid the nobility. Encountering an injured King one day in a strange meeting, Parzival misses the opportunity to bring healing to Wild Mountain. Through a series of unmakings, failed missions, and the news of his deceased mother, it seems that Parzival’s opportunity was forever missed.</p>
            <p>It is not until his journey brings humility and surrender that he could truly seek the path back to Wild Mountain. This journey is one of rejecting a grace offered, engaging in a futile period of searching, and then finally accepting a revelation and awareness of the truth that brings a confession where he “wept with remorse and asked for forgiveness.” From this he emerged a hermit, not a warrior. Immediately the path to return to Wild Mountain, once foretold impossible to find again, was revealed to him. So he returned, and now in complete submission and humility he beheld the wounded King. Instead of maintaining his chivalrous silence as he did before, he responds to the wounded King with a simple compassionate question: “Uncle, what ails you?”</p>
            <p>Through stories like this, Shaw calls us to journey to our eternal home in humility and surrender. It is not something we might simply learn to recite, but a journey we undertake so that we might truly know. One cannot simply “see”; we must “behold.” It is not Parzival’s words that rediscovered Wild Mountain, it was his humble compassion. This is a compassion born from a journey that makes us more conscious of reality, raises our call to participate in God’s story, and humbles us in the face of such a glorious Author. So Shaw exhorts us “to cleave to grace, to not be mastered by the hypnosis of so much social media, to get to know a patch of land, to get to know yourself, your people. To get to know your God.”</p>
            <p>In a world that claims to value authenticity but rewards manufactured personas, we must see the dangers of seeking an “authenticity” that isolates us from our context, place, people, and myth. We must also see the dangers of putting ourselves aside so much so that all of our practices, habits, religious convictions, materials, and loves are like costumes we can put on and take off. Or, in Shaw’s question, how do we really examine what it is we are made of?</p>
            <p>Being made is a journey of many starts, many stops, many unmakings, many offerings of grace, much learning, and much glory. Being made is an act of right worship that recognizes beauty, responds to it, and knows our human limits.</p>
            <p>Under the cover of the dark sky that holds our collective myths, sometimes we delight in the beauty of flickering fireflies and sometimes we anxiously wait for dawn. As we knit our own stories together, the crickets, the frogs, and the owls of the world sing with us to our Creator and Savior a song of praise. In their song they send us off on a journey far away only to welcome us home seventeen years later—years so long, yet they feel like a moment. And it is here I can join Shaw in that liturgy of the wild as a fellow praise-maker.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Emily Carrington is the executive director of Friends of Ashland Theological Seminary.</p>

