          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 13 ====== -->
          <section id="the-political-economy-of" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">14 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The Political Economy of the Spirit</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By Kyle Edward Williams</p>

            <p class="essay-heading">Jordan J. Ballor and Erik W. Matson. <em>A History of Christian Political Economy: From the Patristics to the Present</em>. B&amp;H Academic, 2026. $54.99. 432 pp.</p>
            <p>In a treatise from the early fifth century, Augustine of Hippo tells a story about an actor who engages in a kind of modern performance art <em>avant la lettre.</em> He promises a theater audience that on a particular date in the future he will read their minds and reveal what each of them desires. When the appointed day comes, the audience returns in far greater numbers and with eager expectation. “All were in suspense and silent,” Augustine says. The actor tells them: “You wish to buy cheap and sell dear.”</p>
            <p>Lucky for him, the audience didn’t revolt. The man had spoken wisely, after all. It is a truth widely acknowledged, as Adam Smith put it many centuries later in <em>The Wealth of Nations,</em> that there is a human propensity “to truck, barter, and exchange one thing for another”—all to one’s own advantage. Smith made this propensity for trade the foundation for his theory of the division of labor and his followers would go on to make it the cornerstone of the modern economy.</p>
            <p>As a just-so story for capitalism, it’s about as tidy as any Kipling tale. But it lacks what we might expect from a story written for grownups, which is to say that it lacks an authentic attempt to grapple with the ethical stakes and human complexities of the real world. In order to come to grips with the sphere of activity as vast and varied and consequential as the way we relate to one another in our economic lives, we need a method that is capable of sustaining what might be called criticism. We need a way of thinking that acknowledges the individual person in the larger whole—a method of ethical reasoning that can account for the desires that drive what John Maynard Keynes called the animal spirits as well as the structures of production and trade that shape the fortunes of entire societies. This method is sometimes called political economy.</p>
            <p>So it is a positive turn of events that Jordan J. Ballor, executive director of First Liberty's Center for Religion, Culture and Democracy, and Erik W. Matson, Gibbons Fellow in Economics at the Catholic University of America, have written <em>A History of Christian Political Economy.</em> It is a sweeping survey of Christian thinking about the economy from the early church to the twentieth century. From Basil the Great and before to Frederic Bastiat and beyond, this book convenes a conversation about the role of economic matters in a Christian society. Insofar as it invites readers to explore a genuine tradition of moral reasoning about the economy since the days of the early church, this book is a success.</p>
            <p>Consider, for example, the old theory of a just price. The theory holds that for any given good or service there is a fitting price. Supply and demand say one thing, but justice may say something else entirely. Just price theory, however, did not spring fully formed from the minds of the church fathers. Writers such as Tertullian held a view not at all uncommon among the Romans that commerce was a lowly and largely disreputable activity. “Is trading fit for God?” he asked. “Certainly, if greed is absent, which is the cause of acquisition. But if the acquisition ceases, there will be no longer the necessity of trading.” Leo the Great, similarly: “It is difficult for buyers and sellers not to fall into sin.” But it was Tertullian’s fellow North African, Augustine, who later drew a distinction between the occupation of trade and the manner, whether just or unjust, in which the trader does his business. One key measurement of evaluating the justice of a trader is the price that he offers for his goods.</p>
            <p>The medieval scholastics became interested in theorizing about price, along with almost everything else. Thomas Aquinas would argue that price represents not only the volume of demand but also the input of labor. The story of just price theory takes a few twists and turns: the later schoolmen argued that any given current market price was the norm against which conscience and other considerations should be balanced, and early modern theologians such as Conrad Summenhart and Francisco de Vitoria theorized that the market price was the result of various factors, including human desire and pleasure, which are inherently unstable. In Ballor and Matson’s telling, the story reaches a denouement with John Locke who goes even further than the Hispanic Scholastics. Locke argues that a just price is achieved whenever a transaction is made freely between two consenting parties, period.</p>
            <p>Take another example: usury. The Scriptures are quite clear that usury is a sin: When Jesus tells the crowds to “lend, hoping for nothing again” (Luke 6:35), he is merely applying a well-established dictum from the Hebrew Scriptures. Canon 17 of the Council of Nicaea prescribes the defrocking of a priest for collecting interest out of covetousness. Leo the Great would condemn the practice among the laity. But in the medieval and modern periods, usury was considered by turns 1) an unacceptable practice (following the traditional teaching); 2) permissible under particular conditions (a genuine revision of the traditional teaching); and finally 3), a salutary practice, regardless of the circumstances as long as the interest rate was not exorbitant.</p>
            <p>By 1787, Jeremy Bentham would write <em>A Defense of Usury,</em> with an esteemed Protestant tradition that included John Calvin and Hugo Grotius behind him. Ballor and Matson quote John Wesley who wrote that far from being the root of all evil, money is “an excellent gift of God, answering the noblest ends… it may be as eyes to the blind, as feet to the lame; yea, a lifter up from the gates of death!” From this perspective, perhaps it might be easy to see how forbidding usury only throttles the supply: better to make it more accessible through the business of lending because, as the Boston Puritan Samuel Willard put it, money is “the most fertile thing in the world.”</p>
            <p><em>A History of Christian Political Economy</em> collects ethical and theological reflections not only on just price theory and usury but also on dozens of other topics, including work and the personal use of money, wealth and poverty, property and production, conquest and colonization, and the reductionism of contemporary economic analyses. Published along with a primary source reader, this volume is perhaps the most ambitious of its kind, at least since the publication of Ernst Troeltsch’s <em>The Social Teaching of the Christian Churches</em> (1912).</p>
            <p>The challenging part of writing a sweeping synthesis like this is, of course, the synthesizing part. Assembling such a diverse array of texts, authors, and topics across a period of two millennia is no easy task. Ballor and Matson reckon with their material in two overlapping ways. One that they borrow from the nineteenth-century liberal economist Frederic Bastiat might be imagined along a horizontal axis, running from those who view profit as the loss of the other and those who view profit as the profit of the other—“limited good” versus “mutual benefit.” Across the vertical axis, they supply another contrariety: a thinker who focuses on material matters as opposed to one who foregrounds spiritual goods.</p>
            <p>Two things might be said about these organizing devices, both of them critical. The first is that pitting the material against the spiritual and vice versa is a flawed paradigm, which should be clear to anyone familiar with the teachings of the seven ecumenical councils of the universal church—or, at least, the New Testament.</p>
            <p>The second is that although Ballor and Matson insist that they are not writing a Whig history that might cast, for example, the mutual-benefits perspective as the long-awaited arrival point of the Christian intellectual tradition, it is difficult not to notice that this book presents a history made safe for the Acton Institute. For example, the old radical perspective of the Cappadocians and St. John Chrysostom that denounced the concentration of wealth as the source of poverty and social discord is, in this telling of history, placed within a trajectory that is clearly sidelined, the modern arrival point of which is Christian Socialism or liberation theology. Or, to use the example of usury mentioned already, the fact that many late medieval and early modern Roman Catholics, along with a host of Protestant theologians, had so vivisected traditional teaching about the charging of interest and other forms of financial manipulation as to render it anemic—this might seem worthy of some kind of clear judgment or comment. Instead, readers are led to conclude that this relaxation of ethical expectations was either an inevitable development under the conditions of an emerging modern economy or a genuine advance over what the authors condescendingly call a “longstanding moral taboo.”</p>
            <p>Ballor and Matson save their clearest judgments for the lengthy conclusion where they make the case that theology needs the discipline of economics and vice versa. This is certainly true, but the problem there is the one that runs through the book: the assumption that economics is somehow a neutral field of study. More about that in a moment.</p>
            <p>Generally speaking, however, Ballor and Matson maintain a workmanlike restraint toward offering judgments about what, exactly, a Christian political economy is supposed to be. The book does not purport to present a unified tradition of teaching on the subject. And although this might be an understandable editorial choice given that it is clearly intended as a kind of textbook, it is unfortunate because <em>A History of Christian Political Economy</em> stands more or less as a collection of what important and influential Christian writers have said about explicitly economic matters.</p>
            <p>This is a significant flaw and it is not difficult to see how. Consider that of all the things that the Scriptures have to say about wealth and exchange, perhaps none are more important for understanding the economy as “the earth is the Lord’s and all that therein is” (Psalm 24:1) and “be ye sure that the Lord he is God; it is he that hath made us, and not we ourselves” (Psalm 100:3). Ethical instruction flows from teaching about creation. So, likewise, some of the most important things that Christians have written about political economy are not explicitly about political economy. They are about metaphysics—the character and the nature of the world that God made and continues to sustain.</p>
            <p>An alternative and more normative approach to this topic, then, might begin with a theological and philosophical exploration of the credendum that the world is a gift and that it is ordered toward an ethic of gratuity. The point is not simply that all wealth comes from God with obligations of generosity attached but rather that the structure of exchange itself should be gratuitous in character. The mutual-benefits paradigm that Ballor and Matson glean from Bastiat and, before him, from the “invisible hand” ideology attributed to Adam Smith, promises prosperity in a general, average sort of way within a larger economic structure. The metaphysics of gift, I would argue, calls for something more radical from reciprocity and, perhaps for that reason, more unsettling: that all of our economic relations should be brotherly in character. It is worth nothing, then, that to desire to buy cheap and sell dear is, according to Augustine, a sinful desire.</p>
            <p>Taken as normative, this insight would transform the way that we late moderns think about the economy, including particularly the economists who prefer to consider their discipline an objective science rather than a mode of ethical or political reasoning. Unfortunately, Ballor and Matson likewise treat economics as a neutral method that might benefit from the addition of theological considerations—a “match made in heaven.” But a clearer reckoning with metaphysics might countenance something more substantive.</p>
            <p>For all of this, however, <em>A History of Christian Political Economy</em> is to be commended for giving readers much to think about. For one thing, it is important to reckon with the fact that the economy is not some sort of natural artifact. It is something that human beings make and re-make—and political economy is an important method for understanding it. And, for another, Christian reflection on economic matters is not an invention of modernity. It goes back through the centuries to the fathers and doctors and theologians of the church who attended to matters of material creation precisely because they cared so deeply about matters of the spirit.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Kyle Edward Williams is a fellow at the Institute for Advanced Studies in Culture at the University of Virginia and contributing editor at <em>The Hedgehog Review</em>. He is currently at work on <em>The Unbeaten Bounds: The Parish in the Late Modern World</em>. He is an Anglican priest.</p>

