</section>

          <!-- ====== ARTICLE 14 ====== -->
          <section id="sailing-past-watchful-dragons" class="content-section">
            <p class="rd-essay-n">15 of 15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Sailing Past Watchful Dragons: Reading Homer in an Age of Distraction</h2>
            <p class="essay-author">By SJ Murray</p>

            <p>Twenty-two years ago, I sat on a bench under the shade of a Texas live-oak with Robert Fagles, the beloved Princeton professor who spent thirty years translating Homer and Virgil. The film <em>Troy</em> had just hit theatres, so I asked him what he thought of Hollywood’s reckless adaptation of <em>The Iliad.</em> To my surprise, Robert’s eyes twinkled.</p>
            <p>“Why, Sarah-Jane,” he said. “I think it’s wonderful. The old stories are alive; everyone is talking about them. Homer would be so pleased.”</p>
            <p>This year marks my twenty-fourth teaching <em>The Odyssey</em> to undergraduates at Baylor University. Every semester, students ask me some version of the same question: Why read this ancient poem today? Is it an exercise in history and nostalgia, or is something more urgent at stake? Whenever they ask, my mind drifts back to that warm Texas afternoon discussing the power of the Classics with my mentor. Robert refused to play the gatekeeper. He knew <em>The Iliad</em> and <em>The Odyssey</em> were not trade secrets reserved for specialists. They’re songs about the human condition, meant for anyone willing to listen, in any age.</p>
            <p>About two thousand years ago, the Roman philosopher Seneca mocked pedantic scholars who reduced <em>The Odyssey</em> to debates over whether “Ulysses,” as the Romans called him, was blown off-course “between Italy and Sicily, or outside our known world.” Instead, Seneca urged readers to recognize that all of us suffer from the worldly temptations that lure Homer’s protagonist: “Show me rather, by the example of Ulysses, how I am to love my country, my wife, my father, and how, even after suffering shipwreck, I am to sail towards these ends, honorable as they are,” he cautioned.</p>
            <p>To modern Christians, the idea of seeking moral guidance from pagan literature might feel odd, perhaps even dangerous. However, Augustine offers a landmark defense in <em>On Christian Doctrine:</em> All truth is God’s truth, wherever we find it. Just as the Israelites carried pagan gold out of Egypt, modern readers can harvest the fruit of ancient wisdom and put it to good use, provided we discard the chaff that contradicts sound Christian doctrine.</p>
            <p>Still, some skeptics might ask: why read fiction at all? Why not stick to theology?</p>
            <p>Here, C.S. Lewis offers an essential insight. Writing for <em>The New York Times</em> in 1956, Lewis noted that stories (especially fairy tales or myths) are powerful because they “sneak past the watchful dragons” of our intellect: our cynicism, skepticism, and distaste for being lectured. When presented with an abstract moral principle, most of us are inclined to push back and argue. Stories, on the other hand, sneak past our defenses and smuggle truth in. In making such a claim, Lewis was drawing on a deep well of tradition that stretches back through writers like Chaucer, Marie de France, and Augustine to the Gospels. When the disciples ask Jesus why he speaks in parables, He explains that “the people’s heart has become calloused: they hardly hear with their ears and they have closed their eyes.” Parables bypass those self-inflicted barriers and help us to cultivate ears to hear and eyes to see.</p>
            <p>Since stories lower our defenses so effectively, we must also be careful about which ones we let in. Returning to a time-tested classic allows us to test our character and judgment against a proven gold standard. In a culture driven by screens and constant distraction, reading <em>The Odyssey</em> is the kind of slow, demanding work that requires the patience to sit with the text for hours, resisting the impulse to skim or lean on AI summaries. What draws us in even deeper is the promise of self-discovery. Every time I sit with Odysseus’s flaws, he challenges me to confront my own.</p>
            <p>To show how deep, personal reading works in practice, I’ve chosen three of my favorite episodes from <em>The Odyssey.</em> Year after year, these insights have become trusted anchors that empower me to bypass my own watchful dragons and uncover new wisdom as I sail with Odysseus on the wine-dark sea.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">A Broken Man</h3>
            <p>When audiences watched Christopher Nolan’s adaptation of <em>The Odyssey</em> this summer, many were struck by how broken Odysseus appeared on-screen, visibly burdened by the choices he has made and devouring lotus flowers to forget. For me, this was something the film got right.</p>
            <p>I’ll never forget meeting Odysseus in Book 5, sitting on a rocky headland and weeping as he stares out at the water. Homer’s protagonist bears little resemblance to the great, cunning warrior we get to know in <em>The Iliad,</em> where he devises the ruse of Trojan Horse. At the beginning of <em>The Odyssey,</em> he has lost everything: his home, his loved ones, the companions who followed him into battle. He has hit rock bottom.</p>
            <p>For seven years, Odysseus has been trapped on Ogygia by the nymph Calypso, who offers immortality and endless pleasure. Her name derives from the Greek <em>kalyptein:</em> “to cover, to conceal, to hide.” The etymology is a hint that the island perhaps represents less of a prison and more of a retreat. Odysseus is hidden, but he is also hiding from the world—perhaps even from himself.</p>
            <p>I imagine him consumed by a storm of conflicting emotions: yearning for his wife Penelope, yet terrified of returning to Ithaca. A decade of war followed by the loss of his crew has changed him. Will Penelope welcome him? Will his son respect him? After seven years to reflect on why his journey went disastrously wrong, Odysseus must have spent sleepless nights painfully aware that his pride <em>(hubris)</em> and lack of restraint <em>(akrasia)</em> brought ruin to his men.</p>
            <p>In Ancient Greece, the Pythagoreans viewed seven as a sacred number symbolizing completeness and cosmic order. By Book 5, the time has come for Odysseus to go home; the gods themselves have ordained it. Instead of immortality, Homer’s protagonist chooses a mortal life, filled with brokenness, aging, and accountability. He builds a raft because he would rather endure suffering and death—like us—than spend eternity in someone else’s god-like paradise.</p>
            <p>The next time we see Odysseus weep so woefully is in Book 8, inside the palace of the Phaeacians. Driven ashore by Poseidon, Odysseus spends the night buried in the dirt and leaves of an olive grove, Athena’s sacred tree. When he wakes up, Princess Nausicaa offers him fresh clothing. Odysseus bathes in the river, washing away the encrusted sea salt of Poseidon’s curse—a symbolic moment of rebirth. Under Athena’s protection, he presents himself as a stranger to King Alcinous and Queen Arete, who extend unconditional hospitality <em>(xenia),</em> the “Law of Zeus” referenced in Nolan’s film. In Homeric culture, every stranger comes from Zeus, and they might even be a god in disguise, so it’s best to offer them food, drink, and a bath before asking any questions. In return, the guest is expected to show respect and not abuse their host’s kindness.</p>
            <p>The Phaeacians hold a great feast. That’s when the blind bard Demodocus steps forward to sing of Troy and the Greeks who fell in battle. Odysseus cannot contain his grief; he pulls his cloak over his face to conceal his tears, but everyone notices. He can no longer hide: The bard’s song has forced him to confront his own tragic story from the outside. In this touching moment, Homer invites us to consider a profound truth: When we are lost, art can crack us open and help us find ourselves again. So can community.</p>
            <p>And so, at the beginning of Book 9, the stranger reclaims his identity: “So let me begin by telling you my name…,” he announces, for all to hear. “I am Odysseus, son of Laertes.” The long-wandering hero picks up where Demodocus left off. Rather than emphasize his own heroism or glory, he offers an unvarnished, public confession of his failings.</p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Pride as the Root of Suffering</h3>
            <p>Odysseus makes his moral reckoning clear during the encounter with the Cyclops. In his version of the escape from the cave, he does not blame sea monsters or vengeful gods for the catastrophe. In fact, he spares no detail—not even the least flattering ones.</p>
            <p>The episode complicates the theme of hospitality. In Books 1-4, we witnessed how <em>xenia</em> can be abused by the treacherous suitors in Ithaca. In Book 9, Odysseus and his men enter Polyphemus’s cave uninvited; starving, they help themselves to food, bending divine law to suit their immediate needs. If I found myself in the same situation, I’d probably not think twice about asking for forgiveness later rather than seeking permission, and yet the backdrop Homer has established about <em>xenia</em> raises an unsettling question: Who, exactly, is the monster in this episode?</p>
            <p>Polyphemus certainly looks the part. He traps the intruders and devours them off two by two, like dainty <em>amuse-bouches.</em> Odysseus falls back on his legendary cunning <em>(mêtis).</em> Once the Cyclops is drunk, the Greeks blind him with a stake, then escape by clinging to the woolly underbellies of giant sheep.</p>
            <p>The chain of cause and effect is clear: the famished Greeks plunder the cave first; only then does Polyphemus retaliate. Odysseus and his men threaten the Cyclops’s home and flock. I certainly doubt that Polyphemus would have welcomed them if they had exercised restraint and awaited their host’s return, but we’ll never know. Nolan captures this nuance masterfully in the film when the Greeks realize too late that the Cyclops is capable of speech. “Should we reason with him?” One of them asks. Odysseus replies, “I think we’re past that.”</p>
            <p>Throughout their journey, the Greeks suffer again and again from a fatal lack of self-restraint <em>(akrasia).</em> In the Cyclops episode, Homer highlights this dynamic through a brilliant wordplay. When Polyphemus asks the stranger’s name, Odysseus replies “Nobody” <em>(outis).</em> So when the giant cries out for help and the other Cyclopes ask, “What, Polyphemus, what in the world’s the trouble?” I imagine how confused they must be when Polyphemus screams back: “Nobody, friends. Nobody’s killing me now by fraud and not by force!”</p>
            <p>If only the story ended there, but <em>akrasia</em> is not done with Odysseus yet. Safely aboard his ship, he has to get the last word: “Cyclops,” he yells back across the water, “if any man on the face of the earth should ask you who blinded you, shamed you so, say Odysseus, Raider of Cities. He gauged out your eye. Laertes’ son who makes his home in Ithaca.” That moment—when Odysseus names himself for fame—seals his fate and dooms the entire crew. Had he kept quiet, Polyphemus would not have known who to blame, but now, the Cyclops calls upon his father, the sea god Poseidon, to bring vengeance against this “Odysseus.”</p>
            <p>The lesson is sobering: even brilliant intelligence <em>(mêtis)</em> is useless when it is hijacked by excessive pride <em>(hubris)</em> and led astray by a total lack of restraint <em>(akrasia).</em></p>
            <h3 class="section-heading">Penelope Gets the Last Word</h3>
            <p>If Odysseus and his men succumb to pride and a fatal lack of self-restraint, Penelope epitomizes the opposite virtue: temperance and soundness of mind <em>(sophrosyne).</em></p>
            <p>For twenty years, she relies on her own quiet form of cunning. Every day, she weaves a burial shroud for Odysseus’s elderly father, Laertes, promising the suitors she will remarry when she is done. At night, when no one is watching, she undoes all of her progress. Whereas Odysseus wanted to be known across the whole wide world of men for defeating Polyphemus, Penelope deploys her plan quietly, and in secret, to protect her home, her son, and her marriage. She holds Ithaca together by a thread.</p>
            <p>It is no coincidence that weaving is Athena’s art. Just as the goddess protects Odysseus at war and throughout his journey, she also safeguards Penelope as she wages her own quiet war of resistance against the suitors.</p>
            <p>The greatest test to Penelope’s self-restraint does not come from the chaos the suitors wreak upon the palace. It is Odysseus’s possible return that risks unsettling her. But she makes sure not to give in to wishful thinking: When a beggar arrives at the palace and confirms Odysseus is alive, Penelope treats him with respect but still guards her heart. It’s as though she cannot risk undoing the fragile balance she maintains over herself, and by extension, the kingdom. To me, she exemplifies the Stoic teaching that to govern one’s realm, one must first learn to govern one’s mind.</p>
            <p>Even after Odysseus passes the Test of the Bow and slays the suitors, Penelope’s <em>sophrosyne</em> holds. In private, she subjects him to yet another test by asking him to move their marriage bed. But Odysseus knows their secret: the bed cannot be moved, because it was carved into the roots of a great olive tree, firmly anchored in the earth—a metaphor for the stability of the marriage they are now ready to restore after two long decades of physical separation.</p>
            <p>Penelope’s faithfulness echoes all the way to the Underworld. In Book 24, an ending easily overlooked by modern readers (and by Nolan in the film), the messenger god Hermes leads the shades of the slain suitors down into Hades, where Agamemnon and Achilles greet them. Eager for news from the world above, and curious as to how so many men are arriving all at once, Agammemnon asks how they died: Was it on the battlefield, in glory? Or did they suffer a great shipwreck? The suitor Amphimedon blames Penelope’s trickery for the suitors’ demise, probably expecting to find a sympathetic ear in Agamemnon, who was murdered by his wife Clytemnestra upon his return from the war. But the old king sees right through Amphimedon’s lie and extols Penelope’s virtue:</p>
            <blockquote><p>“Happy Odysseus,” Agammemnon’s ghost<br>cried out, “son of old Laertes— mastermind—<br>what a fine, faithful wife you won! What<br>good sense resided in your Penelope—how<br>well Icarius’s daughter remembered you,<br>Odysseus, the man she married once! The<br>fame of her great virtue will never die. The<br>immortal gods will lift a song, a glorious song<br>in praise of self-possessed Penelope.”</p></blockquote>
            <p>In a culture seemingly obsessed with battlefield glory—an obsession that Achilles denounced to Odysseus when they met in the Underworld in Book 11—Homer reserves the ultimate praise at the end of <em>The Odyssey,</em> and everlasting divine praise, for the woman who stayed home, endured years of loneliness and dishonor, and kept her remarkable restraint <em>(sophrosyne)</em> intact. In Hades, Penelope gets the last word.</p>
            <p>Penelope’s quiet virtue presents a sobering rebuke to our own fast-paced and restless age. When we chase success and worldly honor, we are more like Odysseus crying out to Polyphemus than the patient queen of Ithaca, who never loses hope or her keen sense of purpose. In a world where distraction and instant gratification beckon at every twist and turn, reading <em>The Odyssey</em> demands that we become more like her, planting both feet firmly to stand strong amidst the chaos, like the marriage bed that cannot be moved. When we slow down and allow Homer to lead us through the slow and sometimes uncomfortable work of self-examination, his poem becomes an even more powerful refuge from the storm than Calypso’s island.</p>
            <p>I understand now why Robert Fagles smiled when I asked him about Hollywood adapting Homer all those years ago. He knew that flashy technology and rewriting could not rob a poem like <em>The Iliad</em> or <em>The Odyssey</em> of its ability to speak across the ages to the human soul and guide us back to Homer’s song. We are all trying to find our way home. Provided we cultivate the patience and self-restraint required to move slowly in our chaotic, ever-changing world, the old stories will always be alive, waiting to guide us along our way.</p>
            <p class="contributor-bio">Sarah-Jane Murray is professor of Great Texts and creative writing at Baylor University.</p>
          </section>

